/* generated by tools/video_shotlist_science.py from optima-4th-science/data/videos: rerun after adding a lesson */
window.SHOTLIST={"lessons":[{"w":10,"d":"01","title":"Lesson 10.01: Web of Life","file":"lesson-10-01-web-of-life.html","fileUrl":"../../optima-4th-science/lesson-10-01-web-of-life.html","clips":[{"id":"welcome","label":"Teacher · Welcome · open the case (50–60 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-welcome","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"Hi, scientists! Welcome back to the Field Station. Today’s case starts with a tree.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Before the oak · Before the owl]\n\nThis giant live oak stood in a Florida hammock, a shady patch of forest. A barred owl lived up in its limbs.\n\n[ON SCREEN: After the fallen oak · After the mushrooms]\n\nThen a storm knocked it over. Look what showed up on the trunk. Hmm. Only ONE tree fell, but the owl left, the squirrels left, and mushrooms moved in.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nThat’s our case: why did so much change?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your clue bar]\n\nEvery game you finish adds a clue to “My clues.” Press Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie, right below me.","want":true},{"id":"warmup","label":"Teacher · Warm-Up · tie the threads (Clue 1) (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-warmup","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"Let’s meet the oak’s neighbors.\n\n(Hold up a piece of string.) Scientists draw a line, or a thread, for every connection between living things.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The five threads]\n\nHere are five kinds of threads. Only one is about eating!\n\n[ON SCREEN: How to tie]\n\nTap a visitor to read what it does with the oak. Then drag the thread that fits, or tap the thread and then the visitor. One visitor isn’t even alive. Can you spot it?\n\nWhen all five are tied, list what you found in the boxes. Press Listen. Dr. Reyes has a tip for you.","want":true},{"id":"part3","label":"Teacher · Part 3 · pull a thread (Clue 3) (45–55 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-part3","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"(Hold a string web or a cat’s cradle, then tug one string.) Watch. I pull one string, and look how many others move!\n\n[ON SCREEN: The web]\n\nThis is the web around Dr. Reyes’s oak. Each colored line is one connection.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Predict first]\n\nPick a thread to pull. Before you pull, predict: tap every living thing you think will change. Then press Pull the thread and see how close you were. Try all three.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie. One of these pulls surprised Dr. Reyes!","want":true},{"id":"explore","label":"Teacher · Part 4 · the saw palmetto (Clue 4) (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-explore","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Saw palmetto]\n\nHave you seen this plant? Saw palmetto grows all over Florida. Its stems have tiny teeth, like a saw. Ouch!\n\nIt’s short, but it has a web of its own.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Five boxes]\n\nRead about it, then drag each card into its kind of connection. One card goes in each box. That’s your last clue!\n\nPress Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie first.","want":true},{"id":"assignment","label":"Teacher · Assignment · crack the case (45–55 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-assignment","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nYou found all four clues! At the top of this tab, crack the case: why did so much change when only one tree fell?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your case report]\n\nThen write your case report: two short answers, your own ecosystem web, and a CER.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Help buttons]\n\nWatch the chips light up as you write. Stuck? Press Hint. Your answers save as you type.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":true},{"id":"evidence","label":"Teacher · Use your evidence · CER (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-evidence","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The quote]\n\nSome people say, “As long as we have plants and animals, the small stuff doesn’t matter.” Bees. Mushrooms. Bacteria. Hmm. Think about the threads you pulled.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nRemember CER: your claim, your evidence from today, and your reasoning.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The planner]\n\nTap your side, then two pieces of evidence. They drop right into your boxes. The reasoning is all you. Try starting with “This shows that…”\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes wants to hear your side.","want":true},{"id":"closing","label":"Teacher · Case closed (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-closing","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"Case closed! One tree was the center of a whole web.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your specimen]\n\nFinish your checklist and a live oak acorn goes into your Field Notebook.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Confetti]\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nNext time, we’ll look at real numbers from Florida. How can you tell if a place is healthy, or out of balance?\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s last word.","want":true},{"id":"part1","label":"Teacher · Part 1 · the real live oak (Clue 2) (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-part1","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The live oak photo]\n\nThis is a real live oak, the same kind as Dr. Reyes’s tree. See those little ferns on the limbs? Open every number, then match each kind of connection to a number. That’s Clue 2! Press Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":false},{"id":"quiz","label":"Teacher · Self-Check (15–20 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-quiz","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Quick check: three questions, one at a time. It’s not graded. If you miss one, read the hint and try again.","want":false},{"id":"partc","label":"Teacher · Part C · your own ecosystem web (25–35 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-partc","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Your place]\n\nNow it’s your web. Pick a Florida place you know. Name five living things there, then three connections, each a different kind. Want to draw it? The sketch pad is a bonus.","want":false},{"id":"vocab","label":"Teacher · Word Wall (30–45 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-vocab","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"lesson","label":"Teacher · The Lesson (30–45 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-lesson","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"part2","label":"Teacher · Part 2 · four kinds of connections (30–45 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-part2","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"partb","label":"Teacher · Part B (30 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-1-partb","done":false,"script":""}],"cards":{"sci":{"welcome":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"oak","label":"📷 Before the oak"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"owl","label":"📷 Before the owl"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"fallen","label":"📷 After the fallen oak"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"fungus","label":"📷 After the mushrooms"},{"label":"🗂 The case question","tag":"CASE FILE 10","title":"The Day the Big Oak Fell","col":"#B9822A","body":"Why did so much change when only one tree fell?"},{"label":"🧭 Your clue bar","tag":"HOW TO SOLVE IT","title":"Find 4 clues","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🗂 My clues","The bar under the tabs. Tap it to see your clues."],["? ? ? ?","Each game you finish fills in a clue."],["🔒","Crack the case at the top of the Assignment."]]}],"warmup":[{"label":"🧵 The five threads","tag":"WARM-UP GAME","title":"Five kinds of threads","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🍽️","Food"],["🌼","Pollination"],["🏠","Shelter"],["♻️","Nutrients"],["🤲","Human care"]]},{"label":"👆 How to tie","tag":"HOW TO PLAY","title":"Drag or tap","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["1","Tap a visitor to read what it does."],["2","Drag a thread onto it, or tap the thread, then the visitor."],["3","Tie all five, then list what you found."]]}],"part3":[{"label":"🕸️ The web","tag":"THE WEB AROUND THE OAK","title":"Each line is one connection","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🌳","Live oak in the middle"],["🐿️ 🦉 🐝","Tied to the oak"],["🌴 🐻","Two threads away"]]},{"label":"🔮 Predict first","tag":"HOW TO PLAY","title":"Guess, then pull","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["1","Pick a thread to pull."],["2","Tap the living things you think will change."],["3","Press Pull the thread and check your guess."]]}],"explore":[{"label":"🌴 Saw palmetto","tag":"PART 4","title":"A short palm with fan-shaped fronds","col":"#2F7A55","body":"It grows all over Florida, often right under big oaks."},{"label":"🗃 Five boxes","tag":"THE SORT","title":"One card in each box","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🍽️","Food"],["🌼","Pollination"],["🏠","Shelter"],["♻️","Nutrients"],["🤲","Human care"]]}],"assignment":[{"label":"🔓 Crack the case","tag":"TOP OF THE ASSIGNMENT","title":"Why did so much change?","col":"#B9822A","body":"Pick the answer that fits all four clues."},{"label":"✍️ Your case report","tag":"WHAT TO WRITE","title":"Four places to write","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Part B","Two short answers"],["Part C","Your own ecosystem web"],["CER","Claim, evidence, reasoning"],["📋","Copy your Gathered Answers into Canvas"]]},{"label":"🛟 Help buttons","tag":"STUCK?","title":"Help under every box","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["Your answer needs","Chips light up as you write."],["Hint","Three hints, one at a time."],["📎 My evidence","Your highlights and photo evidence."]]}],"evidence":[{"label":"💬 The quote","tag":"USE YOUR EVIDENCE","title":"Some people say…","col":"#C8553D","body":"“As long as we have plants and animals, we don’t need to worry about the small stuff like bees, fungi, and bacteria.”"},{"label":"⚖ CER","tag":"REMEMBER CER?","title":"Claim · Evidence · Reasoning","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["C","Claim: what you think is true."],["E","Evidence: facts from the lesson that prove it."],["R","Reasoning: how the facts prove your claim."]]},{"label":"🧩 The planner","tag":"PLAN BEFORE YOU WRITE","title":"Tap to plan","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["1","Tap I agree or I disagree."],["2","Tap two pieces of evidence."],["3","They drop into your C and E boxes. You write the R."]]}],"closing":[{"label":"🌰 Your specimen","tag":"FIELD NOTEBOOK","title":"Live oak acorn","col":"#B9822A","body":"Finish the checklist to stamp CASE CLOSED."},{"label":"➡ Next time","tag":"LESSON 10.02","title":"Healthy or out of balance?","col":"#2F7A55","body":"Real Florida numbers."}],"part1":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"liveoak","label":"📸 The live oak photo"}],"quiz":[],"partc":[{"label":"🗺️ Your place","tag":"PART C","title":"Pick a Florida place","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🏖️","A beach"],["🏡","A backyard"],["🐸","A pond"],["🌲","A pine forest"]]}]}},"words":[{"w":"Ecosystem","d":"All the living things and nonliving things — like soil, water, and air — in one place, working together.","e":"🌳"},{"w":"Habitat","d":"The place where a living thing finds food, water, shelter, and space. The oak tree is a habitat for many small animals.","e":"🏡"},{"w":"Pollination","d":"When pollen moves from one flower to another so seeds can form. Bees, butterflies, and even wind can pollinate plants.","e":"🐝"},{"w":"Pollinator","d":"An animal that carries pollen from flower to flower. Bees, butterflies, hummingbirds, and even bats can be pollinators.","e":"🦋"},{"w":"Nutrients","d":"The materials in soil and food that plants and animals need to grow and stay healthy.","e":"🟤"},{"w":"Web of life","d":"All the connections — eating, pollinating, sheltering, breaking down — that tie living things together in an ecosystem.","e":"🕸️"}]},{"w":10,"d":"02","title":"Lesson 10.02: Ecosystem Balance","file":"lesson-10-02-ecosystem-balance.html","fileUrl":"../../optima-4th-science/lesson-10-02-ecosystem-balance.html","clips":[{"id":"welcome","label":"Teacher · Welcome · the one-year jump (50–60 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-2-welcome","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"Hi, scientists! Last time, one fallen oak changed a whole forest. Today: how do scientists know if a whole ecosystem is healthy?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Manatees from the air]\n\nEvery winter, scientists fly over Florida in small planes and count manatees. Those gray shapes? Manatees!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two flight logs]\n\nLook at these two flight logs. 2009: 3,802. One year later: 5,077. That’s more than twelve hundred extra manatees in one year. Hmm. Manatees usually have one baby every few years.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nSo did that many really show up? Find the four clues. Press Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie, right below me.","want":true},{"id":"warmup","label":"Teacher · Warm-Up · count it or feel it? (Clue 1) (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-2-warmup","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"(Step outside or look out a window.) Is it a nice day? I think so. But you might not! That’s a feeling.\n\nHow many clouds are in the sky? That we can count.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two sides]\n\nScientists trust things they can count or measure, not feelings.\n\n[ON SCREEN: How to play]\n\nRead each card about two Florida wetlands. Drag it to the side it belongs on, or tap the side. Then list three things a scientist could measure. Press Listen. Dr. Reyes has a field secret for you.","want":true},{"id":"part2","label":"Teacher · Part 2 · fly the survey (Clue 2) (45–55 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-2-part2","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Manatees in a warm spring]\n\nWhen the water gets cold, manatees crowd into warm springs like this one. Easy to count! On warm days they spread out all over.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The practice flight]\n\nNow you fly. Two hundred manatees live near this spring, every single day. Pick the weather and the wind, then fly. Write down your count. Now change just one thing and fly again.\n\nDid the manatees change, or did your count? That’s Clue 2. Press Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":true},{"id":"explore","label":"Teacher · Part 4 · thirty years of counts (Clue 4) (45–55 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-2-explore","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"(Hold up one sock.) If I find one sock on the floor, is my room messy? Maybe! I need more evidence.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two dots]\n\nThis chart starts with just two survey flights, one year apart. Up or down? Decide.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Every flight]\n\nThen press Show every flight. Thirty years of real dots! Some years jump, some dip. The dashed line shows the trend, the way the numbers move over many years.\n\nThat’s your last clue. Press Listen.","want":true},{"id":"assignment","label":"Teacher · Assignment · crack the case (45–55 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-2-assignment","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nYou found all four clues! Crack the case at the top of this tab. Did twelve hundred manatees really appear in one year?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your case report]\n\nThen your case report: two short answers, a graph, and a CER.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Part C graph]\n\nIn Part C, you build the graph. Tap in each column to set the dot at the right height. Then write one sentence: which way did the numbers go?\n\nStuck? Press Hint under any box. Press Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":true},{"id":"evidence","label":"Teacher · Use your evidence · CER (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-2-evidence","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: A Burmese python]\n\nThis snake is not from Florida. It came from Asia.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The quote]\n\nSome people say invasive species are no big deal, because Florida has plenty of native animals. Hmm. Do you agree?\n\n[ON SCREEN: The planner]\n\nRemember CER. Tap your side, then two facts from the lesson. Numbers make strong evidence! Then explain how your facts prove your claim. Try starting with “This shows that…”\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes wants to hear your side.","want":true},{"id":"closing","label":"Teacher · Case closed (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-2-closing","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"Case closed! One count can fool you. Many years of counts tell the truth.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your specimen]\n\nFinish your checklist and a Florida apple snail shell goes into your Field Notebook.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Confetti]\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nNext time, we zoom in. Why does a spoonbill have a flat, spoon-shaped beak? We’ll find out how body parts help living things survive.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s last word.","want":true},{"id":"part3","label":"Teacher · Part 3 · four Florida stories (Clue 3) (25–35 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-2-part3","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: A Florida manatee]\n\nFour Florida stories, four case files. Read each one, stamp it Recovering or Out of balance, then find the line that proves it. Careful: some lines are true but don’t prove anything. Press Listen.","want":false},{"id":"quiz","label":"Teacher · Self-Check (15–20 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-2-quiz","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Quick check: three questions, one at a time. It’s not graded. If you miss one, read the hint and try again.","want":false},{"id":"vocab","label":"Teacher · Word Wall (30–45 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-2-vocab","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"lesson","label":"Teacher · The Lesson (30–45 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-2-lesson","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"part1","label":"Teacher · Part 1 · balance (20–30 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-2-part1","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"partb","label":"Teacher · Part B (30 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-2-partb","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"partc","label":"Teacher · Part C · your manatee graph (30 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-10-2-partc","done":false,"script":""}],"cards":{"sci":{"welcome":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"aerial","label":"📷 Manatees from the air"},{"label":"🗂 Two flight logs","tag":"CASE FILE 10 · PART 2","title":"The One-Year Jump","col":"#B9822A","rows":[["Jan 2009","3,802 manatees counted"],["Jan 2010","5,077 manatees counted"]]},{"label":"❓ The case question","tag":"THE QUESTION","title":"Did 1,275 manatees really show up in one year?","col":"#B9822A","body":"Find 4 clues. Then crack the case on the Assignment tab."}],"warmup":[{"label":"⚖ Two sides","tag":"WARM-UP GAME","title":"Count it or feel it?","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["📏","A scientist can count or measure it."],["💭","It’s just a feeling."]]},{"label":"👆 How to play","tag":"HOW TO PLAY","title":"One card at a time","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["1","Read the card."],["2","Drag it to a side, or tap a side."],["3","Then list three things you could measure."]]}],"part2":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"springs","label":"📷 Manatees in a warm spring"},{"label":"🛩 The practice flight","tag":"FLY THE SURVEY","title":"Same 200 manatees every flight","col":"#2D7AA6","rows":[["🌡","Pick the weather."],["🌬","Pick the wind."],["✈","Fly, then change ONE thing and fly again."]]}],"explore":[{"label":"📈 Two dots","tag":"PART 4","title":"Start with two flights","col":"#2D7AA6","rows":[["2010","One flight"],["2011","One year later"]]},{"label":"📈 Every flight","tag":"THE BIG PICTURE","title":"Thirty years of dots","col":"#2D7AA6","body":"The dashed line shows the trend: which way the numbers go over many years."}],"assignment":[{"label":"🔓 Crack the case","tag":"TOP OF THE ASSIGNMENT","title":"Did 1,275 manatees really show up?","col":"#B9822A","body":"Pick the answer that fits all four clues."},{"label":"✍️ Your case report","tag":"WHAT TO DO","title":"Four places to work","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Part B","Two short answers"],["Part C","Build your own manatee graph"],["CER","Claim, evidence, reasoning"],["📋","Copy your Gathered Answers into Canvas"]]},{"label":"📈 Part C graph","tag":"PART C","title":"Tap to place each dot","col":"#2D7AA6","rows":[["1991","About 1,200 counted"],["Today","More than 7,500"],["✏","One sentence about the trend"]]}],"evidence":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"python","label":"📷 A Burmese python"},{"label":"💬 The quote","tag":"USE YOUR EVIDENCE","title":"Some people say…","col":"#C8553D","body":"“Invasive species like Burmese pythons are no big deal. Florida has plenty of native animals.”"},{"label":"🧩 The planner","tag":"PLAN BEFORE YOU WRITE","title":"Claim · Evidence · Reasoning","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["1","Tap I agree or I disagree."],["2","Tap two pieces of evidence."],["3","You write the reasoning."]]}],"closing":[{"label":"🐚 Your specimen","tag":"FIELD NOTEBOOK","title":"Florida apple snail shell","col":"#B9822A","body":"Finish the checklist to stamp CASE CLOSED."},{"label":"➡ Next time","tag":"LESSON 11.01","title":"Inside out","col":"#2F7A55","body":"Body parts that help Florida plants and animals survive."}],"part3":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"manatee","label":"📷 A Florida manatee"}],"quiz":[]}},"words":[{"w":"Native species","d":"A plant or animal that has lived in a place for a very long time and belongs there — like the gopher tortoise or saw palmetto in Florida.","e":"🌴"},{"w":"Invasive species","d":"A plant or animal that doesn't belong in a place and causes harm to the local ecosystem — like the Burmese python in the Everglades.","e":"🐍"},{"w":"Population","d":"All the living things of one kind in one place — like \"the manatee population in the Crystal River.\"","e":"🔢"},{"w":"Balance","d":"When an ecosystem has the right mix of living things and resources for everyone to keep going. A balanced ecosystem can handle small changes.","e":"⚖️"},{"w":"Conservation","d":"Taking care of land, water, plants, and animals so they keep going. Florida's manatee laws are a kind of conservation.","e":"🛡️"},{"w":"Human impact","d":"Any change people cause in an ecosystem — building, farming, polluting, or protecting. Some impacts help. Some hurt.","e":"🚜"}]},{"w":11,"d":"01","title":"Lesson 11.01: Inside Out: Plant and Animal Structures","file":"lesson-11-01-inside-out-structures.html","fileUrl":"../../optima-4th-science/lesson-11-01-inside-out-structures.html","clips":[{"id":"welcome","label":"Teacher · Welcome · the washed-out notebook (50–60 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-1-welcome","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Hi, scientists! Today’s case starts with a soggy notebook.\n\n(Hold up a notebook.) Dr. Reyes found one like this on a boardwalk, soaked by the rain. Only four photos survived.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The beak · Beak in the water]\n\nLook at that beak. It’s flat, like a spoon!\n\n[ON SCREEN: The legs · Where I found it]\n\nLong pink legs, standing in shallow water.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nThe last line is washed out. This bird finds food it can’t even see… how?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your clue bar]\n\nEvery game you finish adds a clue. Press Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie, right below me.","want":true},{"id":"warmup","label":"Teacher · Warm-Up · beak or tool (Clue 1) (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-1-warmup","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"(Hold up a spoon and a straw.) Which one would you use to scoop soup? Which one to sip a drink? The shape tells you the job!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Four tools]\n\nBird beaks work the same way. Here are four tools.\n\n[ON SCREEN: How to match]\n\nLook at each beak’s shape. Drag the tool with the same shape onto it, or tap the tool and then the beak. Then write your best guess: why is a flat beak good for sweeping?\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes has a tip for you.","want":true},{"id":"part2","label":"Teacher · Part 2 · inside or outside (Clue 2) (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-1-part2","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"(Point to your skin, then to your heart.) My skin is external. I can see it. My heart is internal. It’s hidden inside.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two sides]\n\nSort each card. Ask: could I see this part from the outside? Watch the last card. It’s a clue! Press Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":false},{"id":"part3","label":"Teacher · Part 3 · how deep can you go (Clue 3) (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-1-part3","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"(Hold a ruler up to your leg.) If I stood in water this deep, my knees would stay dry. Up to here? I’d be swimming!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Four depths]\n\nTap a water depth and watch the water rise.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Three birds]\n\nThree birds, three kinds of legs. Who can still stand and feed? Try every depth, then answer the question.\n\nPress Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie first.","want":true},{"id":"explore","label":"Teacher · Part 4 · build a hiding head (Clue 4) (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-1-explore","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"(Cover your mouth and nose with your hand.) Can you breathe? Not easily!\n\n[ON SCREEN: A gator hiding]\n\nNow look at this alligator. Its whole body is hiding underwater. What’s still sticking out?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Build and test]\n\nBuild a model head. Put the eyes and nostrils where you want, then sink it. Can it watch? Can it breathe? Test at least two designs. That’s your last clue!\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":true},{"id":"quiz","label":"Teacher · Self-Check (15–20 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-1-quiz","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Quick check: three questions, one at a time. It’s not graded. If you miss one, read the hint and try again.","want":false},{"id":"assignment","label":"Teacher · Assignment · crack the case (45–55 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-1-assignment","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nYou found all four clues! At the top of this tab, crack the case: how does the mystery bird find food it can’t see?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your case report]\n\nThen write your case report: two short answers, a Florida plant or animal with three of its structures, and a CER.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Help buttons]\n\nWatch the chips light up as you write. Stuck? Press Hint. Your answers save as you type.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":true},{"id":"partc","label":"Teacher · Part C · label a Florida organism (25–35 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-1-partc","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Your pick]\n\nNow it’s your turn. Pick a Florida plant or animal, but not the spoonbill. Name three of its structures, the job of each one, and whether it’s internal or external. Want to draw it? The sketch pad is a bonus.","want":false},{"id":"evidence","label":"Teacher · Use your evidence · CER (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-1-evidence","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The quote]\n\nA classmate says plants don’t really have structures. Only animals have body parts that matter. Hmm!\n\n(Hold up a leaf or a plant.) What does this leaf do? What about the roots underneath?\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nRemember CER? Claim, evidence, reasoning.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The planner]\n\nTap your side, tap two pieces of evidence, then write your reasoning in your own words.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes.","want":true},{"id":"closing","label":"Teacher · Case closed (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-1-closing","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Case closed! The spoonbill finds food by feeling, not seeing. Every part fits its job.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your specimen]\n\nFinish your checklist and a pink spoonbill feather goes into your Field Notebook.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Confetti]\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nNext time, you’ll build a model, a drawing that explains how a body part does its job. Bring your clues!\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s last word.","want":true},{"id":"vocab","label":"Teacher · Word Wall (30–45 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-1-vocab","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"lesson","label":"Teacher · The Lesson (30–45 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-1-lesson","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"part1","label":"Teacher · Part 1 · structure meets function (20–30 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-1-part1","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"partb","label":"Teacher · Part B (30 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-1-partb","done":false,"script":""}],"cards":{"sci":{"welcome":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"beak","label":"📷 The beak"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"sweep","label":"📷 Beak in the water"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"legs","label":"📷 The legs"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"lagoon","label":"📷 Where I found it"},{"label":"🗂 The case question","tag":"CASE FILE 11","title":"The Washed-Out Notebook","col":"#B9822A","body":"How does this bird find food it can’t even see?"},{"label":"🧭 Your clue bar","tag":"HOW TO SOLVE IT","title":"Find 4 clues","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🗂 My clues","The bar under the tabs. Tap it to see your clues."],["? ? ? ?","Each game you finish fills in a clue."],["🔒","Crack the case at the top of the Assignment."]]}],"warmup":[{"label":"🔧 Four tools","tag":"WARM-UP GAME","title":"Beak or tool?","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🥄","Spoon"],["🥤","Straw"],["🗜️","Nutcracker"],["🍢","Skewer (a sharp stick)"]]},{"label":"👆 How to match","tag":"HOW TO PLAY","title":"Drag or tap","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["1","Look at the shape of each beak."],["2","Drag a tool onto it, or tap the tool, then the beak."],["3","Match all four, then write your best guess."]]}],"part2":[{"label":"🫀 Two sides","tag":"PART 2 GAME","title":"Inside or outside?","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["👀","External: you can see it from outside."],["🫀","Internal: hidden inside the body."]]}],"part3":[{"label":"💧 Four depths","tag":"PART 3 GAME","title":"How deep can you go?","col":"#2B7BB9","rows":[["💧","1 inch"],["💧","6 inches"],["💧","1 foot"],["💧","3 feet"]]},{"label":"🦵 Three birds","tag":"WHO CAN STAND?","title":"Watch all three","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Sanderling","Short legs"],["Roseate spoonbill","Long legs"],["Mottled duck","Short legs, webbed feet"]]}],"explore":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"gator","label":"📷 A gator hiding"},{"label":"🧪 Build and test","tag":"PART 4 GAME","title":"Build a hiding head","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["1","Pick where the eyes and nostrils go."],["2","Press Sink it to hide."],["3","Read the three tests. Try another design."]]}],"quiz":[],"assignment":[{"label":"🔓 Crack the case","tag":"TOP OF THE ASSIGNMENT","title":"How does it find food?","col":"#B9822A","body":"Pick the answer that fits all four clues."},{"label":"✍️ Your case report","tag":"WHAT TO WRITE","title":"Four places to write","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Part B","Two short answers"],["Part C","A Florida plant or animal and three structures"],["CER","Claim, evidence, reasoning"],["📋","Copy your Gathered Answers into Canvas"]]},{"label":"🛟 Help buttons","tag":"STUCK?","title":"Help under every box","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["Your answer needs","Chips light up as you write."],["Hint","Three hints, one at a time."]]}],"partc":[{"label":"🐾 Your pick","tag":"PART C","title":"Pick a Florida plant or animal","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🐢","A gopher tortoise"],["🦭","A manatee"],["🌴","A cabbage palm"],["🐸","A tree frog"]]}],"evidence":[{"label":"💬 The quote","tag":"USE YOUR EVIDENCE","title":"A classmate says…","col":"#C8553D","body":"“Plants don’t really have structures — they’re just leaves and stems. Only animals have body parts that matter.”"},{"label":"⚖ CER","tag":"REMEMBER CER?","title":"Claim · Evidence · Reasoning","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["C","Claim: what you think is true."],["E","Evidence: facts from the lesson that prove it."],["R","Reasoning: how the facts prove your claim."]]},{"label":"🧩 The planner","tag":"PLAN BEFORE YOU WRITE","title":"Tap to plan","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["1","Tap I agree or I disagree."],["2","Tap two pieces of evidence."],["3","They drop into your C and E boxes. You write the R."]]}],"closing":[{"label":"🪶 Your specimen","tag":"FIELD NOTEBOOK","title":"Roseate spoonbill feather","col":"#B9822A","body":"Finish the checklist to stamp CASE CLOSED."},{"label":"➡ Next time","tag":"LESSON 11.02","title":"Build a model","col":"#2F7A55","body":"A drawing that explains how a body part does its job."}]}},"words":[{"w":"Structure","d":"A body part of a plant or animal. Roots, leaves, beaks, eyes, and shells are all structures.","e":"🦴"},{"w":"Function","d":"The job that a structure does. Leaves function by catching sunlight to make food.","e":"⚙️"},{"w":"Organism","d":"Any single living thing — a plant, an animal, a fungus, a bacterium. One tree is one organism.","e":"🌿"},{"w":"Survival","d":"Staying alive. Structures help an organism survive by helping it find food, water, shelter, or safety from danger.","e":"💪"},{"w":"Internal","d":"Inside the body. A heart, a stomach, and the inside of a stem are all internal structures.","e":"🫀"},{"w":"External","d":"Outside the body, on the surface. Skin, fur, feathers, shells, leaves, and bark are all external structures.","e":"👀"}]},{"w":11,"d":"02","title":"Lesson 11.02: Model: Structure and Survival","file":"lesson-11-02-model-structure-survival.html","fileUrl":"../../optima-4th-science/lesson-11-02-model-structure-survival.html","clips":[{"id":"welcome","label":"Teacher · Welcome · the refuge sign (50–60 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-2-welcome","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Welcome back, scientists! Remember the soggy notebook? Our case report made it to the refuge, and now they want a sign about the spoonbill.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Draft A · Draft B · Draft C]\n\nThree people sent drafts. A photo with labels. A beautiful painting from 1836. And a photo with arrows.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nOnly one can go on the sign. Which one really explains how the spoonbill survives?\n\nFind four clues today. Press Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie, right below me.","want":true},{"id":"warmup","label":"Teacher · Warm-Up · point the arrow (Clue 1) (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-2-warmup","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"(Hold up a sticky note that says “teeth.”) If I stick this on an alligator drawing, what do you learn? Just a name.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The teeth]\n\nNow watch. (Draw an arrow in the air toward a pretend fish.) An arrow points from the part to what it acts on.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two drawings]\n\nBuild Drawing 2: drag the arrow, then pick the action words. Then tell me which drawing teaches a friend more.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s field trick.","want":true},{"id":"part2","label":"Teacher · Part 2 · upgrade the drawing (Clue 2) (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-2-part2","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The anhinga model]\n\nLook at this anhinga. Every arrow goes from a part to what it does. The words at the bottom say why it matters.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Three steps]\n\nNow you upgrade a spoonbill photo, one step at a time: a label, an arrow, a caption. Watch the meter fill up as your drawing turns into a model.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":true},{"id":"part3","label":"Teacher · Part 3 · chain it (Clue 3) (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-2-part3","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The chain]\n\nEvery strong model follows a chain: the part, what it does, and the need it meets. Build four chains. Careful: two cards in each set are tricks. Press Listen first.","want":false},{"id":"explore","label":"Teacher · Part 4 · the model kit (Clue 4) (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-2-explore","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"(Hold up a photo or a picture from a book.) Scientists don’t always draw from scratch. Sometimes they draw arrows right on a photo!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Model kit]\n\nPick a Florida organism: a tree frog, a gopher tortoise, an anhinga or a manatee. Tap a part, point the arrow, and pick what it does and which need it meets. Your caption writes itself. Build two if you can!\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":true},{"id":"quiz","label":"Teacher · Self-Check (15–20 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-2-quiz","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Quick check: three questions, one at a time. It’s not graded. If you miss one, read the hint and try again.","want":false},{"id":"assignment","label":"Teacher · Assignment · crack the case (45–55 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-2-assignment","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nYou found all four clues! At the top of this tab, crack the case: which draft goes on the refuge sign, and what would fix the other two?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your case report]\n\nThen write your case report. In Part C, you build your own model in words: a label, an arrow, a caption. Watch the chips light up. Stuck? Press Hint.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":true},{"id":"partc","label":"Teacher · Part C · build an annotated model (25–35 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-2-partc","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Your model]\n\nPick a Florida organism, not the spoonbill. Name one part, tell what it does, and finish with a caption about a survival need. Want to draw it too? The sketch pad is a bonus.","want":false},{"id":"evidence","label":"Teacher · Use your evidence · CER (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-2-evidence","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The quote]\n\nA classmate says drawings are just art, not science. Hmm!\n\n(Hold up a notebook page with a sketch and arrows.) Is this art, or is it explaining something?\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nPick your side in the planner, tap two pieces of evidence, then write your reasoning in your own words.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes.","want":true},{"id":"closing","label":"Teacher · Case closed (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-2-closing","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Case closed for good! Draft C goes on the sign, because it explains, not just names.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your specimen]\n\nFinish your checklist and an annotated spoonbill sketch goes into your Field Notebook.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Confetti]\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next week]\n\nNext week: four trail cameras catch four Florida animals reacting at once. Why? Bring your detective eyes!\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s last word.","want":true},{"id":"vocab","label":"Teacher · Word Wall (30–45 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-2-vocab","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"lesson","label":"Teacher · The Lesson (30–45 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-2-lesson","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"part1","label":"Teacher · Part 1 · a model explains (20–30 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-2-part1","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"partb","label":"Teacher · Part B (30 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-11-2-partb","done":false,"script":""}],"cards":{"sci":{"welcome":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"draftA","label":"📷 Draft A"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"draftB","label":"📷 Draft B"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"draftC","label":"📷 Draft C"},{"label":"🗂 The case question","tag":"CASE FILE 11 · PART 2","title":"The Refuge Sign","col":"#B9822A","body":"Which draft explains how the spoonbill survives, and what would make the other two better?"}],"warmup":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"gator","label":"📷 The teeth"},{"label":"➜ Two drawings","tag":"WARM-UP GAME","title":"Label or model?","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Drawing 1","just the word “teeth”"],["Drawing 2","an arrow + action words"]]}],"part2":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"anhinga","label":"📷 The anhinga model"},{"label":"⬆️ Three steps","tag":"PART 2 GAME","title":"Upgrade the drawing","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["1","A label"],["2","An arrow that shows action"],["3","A caption about a survival need"]]}],"part3":[{"label":"🔗 The chain","tag":"PART 3 GAME","title":"Structure → Action → Survival need","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🦴","Structure: the part"],["⚙️","Action: what it does"],["🍎","Survival need: food, water, air, shelter or safety"]]}],"explore":[{"label":"🧩 Model kit","tag":"PART 4 GAME","title":"Build a model on a real photo","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["+","Tap a part"],["➜","Point the arrow"],["⚙️","Pick what it does"],["🍎","Pick the survival need"]]}],"quiz":[],"assignment":[{"label":"🔓 Crack the case","tag":"TOP OF THE ASSIGNMENT","title":"Which draft goes on the sign?","col":"#B9822A","body":"Pick the answer that fits all four clues."},{"label":"✍️ Your case report","tag":"WHAT TO WRITE","title":"Four places to write","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Part B","Two short answers"],["Part C","Your own model in words"],["CER","Claim, evidence, reasoning"],["📋","Copy your Gathered Answers into Canvas"]]}],"partc":[{"label":"🏷️ Your model","tag":"PART C","title":"Label · Arrow · Caption","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🏷️","Name the part"],["➜","Tell what it does"],["💬","Tie it to a survival need"]]}],"evidence":[{"label":"💬 The quote","tag":"USE YOUR EVIDENCE","title":"A classmate says…","col":"#C8553D","body":"“A drawing isn’t science — it’s art. Real scientists only use numbers and graphs.”"},{"label":"⚖ CER","tag":"REMEMBER CER?","title":"Claim · Evidence · Reasoning","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["C","Claim: what you think is true."],["E","Evidence: facts from the lesson that prove it."],["R","Reasoning: how the facts prove your claim."]]}],"closing":[{"label":"📝 Your specimen","tag":"FIELD NOTEBOOK","title":"Annotated spoonbill sketch","col":"#B9822A","body":"Finish the checklist to stamp CASE CLOSED."},{"label":"➡ Next week","tag":"LESSON 12.01","title":"Senses to brain","col":"#2F7A55","body":"Four trail cameras, four Florida animals, one mystery."}]}},"words":[{"w":"Model","d":"A way to show how something works. A model can be a drawing, a diagram, a 3-D build, or even an explanation. A good model explains a relationship — not just names parts.","e":"🧩"},{"w":"Function","d":"The job that a structure does. Leaves function by catching sunlight to make food.","e":"⚙️"},{"w":"Adaptation","d":"A structure or trait that helps an organism survive in the place where it lives. A gecko's sticky toes are an adaptation for climbing.","e":"🦎"},{"w":"Survival need","d":"Something every living thing must have: food, water, air, shelter, and safety. Structures help an organism meet these needs.","e":"🍎"},{"w":"Annotate","d":"To add labels, arrows, and short notes to a drawing or diagram so it explains itself.","e":"✏️"},{"w":"Camouflage","d":"A color or pattern that helps an animal blend into its surroundings. A Florida green tree frog's color is a kind of camouflage.","e":"🐸"}]},{"w":12,"d":"01","title":"Lesson 12.01: Sensing the World","file":"lesson-12-01-sensing-the-world.html","fileUrl":"../../optima-4th-science/lesson-12-01-sensing-the-world.html","clips":[{"id":"welcome","label":"Teacher · Welcome · open the case (50–60 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-welcome","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"Hi, scientists! Welcome to the Field Station. Today you're solving a real mystery.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CAM 1 beach · CAM 2 woods · CAM 3 river · CAM 4 marsh]\n\nThese pictures came from four trail cameras in Florida: a baby sea turtle, a panther, a manatee and a cottonmouth snake.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The times]\n\nLook at the clocks. All four animals moved within about two minutes, and the cameras were far apart. Hmm. I wonder why.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nThat's our case: what made them all react, and how did each one know?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your clue bar]\n\nSee \"My clues\" under the tabs? Every game you finish turns a question mark into a clue. Four clues cracks the case.\n\nType your first name on the ID card. Then press Listen on Dr. Reyes's walkie-talkie, right below me.","want":true},{"id":"warmup","label":"Teacher · Warm-Up · the snap test (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-warmup","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"Let's start with you, because your body does this too.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The snap test]\n\nUnder the walkie-talkie is a game called \"Hear the snap.\" Tap the picture, then wait. Don't guess! When you hear the snap, tap fast. Try it three times. Watch me.\n\n(Do the test once on camera. Read your time out loud.)\n\nI didn't stop and think. My body just did it!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your steps]\n\nThen write what happened in your body, in order, from the sound to your tap. There are no wrong answers yet.\n\nHow did your body decide that fast? Press Listen and hear what Dr. Reyes thinks.","want":true},{"id":"vocab","label":"Teacher · Word Wall · act it out (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-vocab","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Four of today's words are steps, so let's act them out. Stand up if you can!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Stimulus]\n\nStimulus: something happens. (Snap your fingers.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Sense organ]\n\nSense organ: a body part notices it. (Point to your ear.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Brain]\n\nBrain: it decides. (Tap your head.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Response]\n\nResponse: you do something. (Turn around.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Act it out]\n\nFaster: snap, ear, head, turn! That's your snap test.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Word Lens]\n\nNow tap each word sticker below me. Dr. Reyes reads the word and what it means. Get a green check on all six.","want":true},{"id":"lesson","label":"Teacher · The Lesson · what’s on this tab (25–30 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-lesson","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: This tab]\n\nThis tab has four parts and three clues. Part 1 teaches the four steps, then you play Send the Signal for clue two. Part 2 is about different senses, with an alligator picture. In Part 3 you open Dr. Reyes's four cameras for clue three. And in Part 4, one more camera shows a heron. Sort its steps for clue four. Go in order, and watch your clue bar.","want":false},{"id":"part1","label":"Teacher · Part 1 · the four steps + Send the Signal (50–60 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-part1","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"Let's name what happened in your snap test.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The four steps]\n\nEvery reaction has four steps, in order. A stimulus: the snap. A sense organ notices it: your ears. Your brain decides: \"Something's behind me!\" And the response: you tapped. All four can happen in less than one second!\n\nThink about your warm-up list. Did you write a step for your brain? Lots of people skip it, because it's so fast.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Send the signal]\n\nBelow the reading is Send the Signal. Drag each step onto a station, starting out in the world. Get all four right and watch the signal run. That's clue two!\n\nFirst, press Listen. Dr. Reyes has something to say about the middle steps.","want":true},{"id":"part2","label":"Teacher · Part 2 · different senses (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-part2","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"An eagle spots a fish with its eyes. A panther hears a rustle with its ears.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Five senses]\n\nEach sense organ picks up something different. Eyes pick up light, ears pick up sound, your nose picks up smell, your tongue picks up taste, and your skin picks up touch, heat and cold.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Watch out]\n\nWatch out for one mistake: the brain can't notice anything by itself. No sense organ, no signal. No signal, no response. Remember that for the end of the assignment.","want":false},{"id":"figure","label":"Teacher · The alligator picture (25–35 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-figure","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Alligator pathway]\n\nRead this picture left to right, like a sentence.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Read it left to right]\n\nA splash: that's the stimulus. The alligator's ear: the sense organ. Its brain decides, \"Food!\" Then it turns toward the splash: the response. The yellow lines show the message traveling. Can you say all four steps without looking?","want":true},{"id":"part3","label":"Teacher · Part 3 · open the four cameras (Clue 3) (45–55 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-part3","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"Time to look at the evidence, like a real field scientist.\n\n[ON SCREEN: How to open a camera]\n\nBelow the walkie-talkie are four trail cameras. Tap one, then press Next step to follow the message through that animal, one step at a time.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CAM 1 beach]\n\nFor each one, ask: what started this reaction? Was it the same thing for all four? That's clue three.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The manatee photo]\n\nThen tap the numbers on the manatee photo to see how it feels the water.\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes will tell you what to watch for.","want":true},{"id":"explore","label":"Teacher · Part 4 · the heron on CAM 5 (Clue 4) (50–60 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-explore","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The heron]\n\nCAM 5 caught a great blue heron hunting, one minute after the other cameras.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The heron story]\n\nRead the story slowly. The heron stands still. A fish swims by. The heron freezes, then strikes. I notice it froze first. I wonder why?\n\n[ON SCREEN: One card, sorted]\n\nDrag each card into its step. Let's do one together: \"A small fish swims into view.\" That happens out in the water, before the heron does anything. So it's the stimulus! You sort the rest.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The hidden step]\n\nThe pink box is the brain. No card goes there, because no camera can see inside the heron's head. Remember that for your CER.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes.","want":true},{"id":"quiz","label":"Teacher · Self-Check · how it works (20 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-quiz","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Self-Check]\n\nThis Self-Check isn't graded. It's just for you. Answer one question, then press Next question. Picked a wrong answer? Read the hint. It tells you what to think about. Think about the four steps, and try again.","want":false},{"id":"assignment","label":"Teacher · Assignment · crack the case + roadmap (45–55 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-assignment","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"You made it to the assignment!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nFirst, crack the case. Still locked? Tap \"Show me where the clues are,\" then \"Go there.\" With all four clues, pick the answer that fits every clue. Did all four animals react to the same thing?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Today’s parts]\n\nThen there are three parts. Part B: two answers in complete sentences. Part C: make up your own Florida animal moment. A sketch is a bonus. And last, a CER.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Saved for you]\n\nEverything saves itself and shows up in Gathered Answers at the bottom. That's what you turn in.\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes wants a real field report.","want":true},{"id":"partb","label":"Teacher · Part B · short answers (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-partb","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Part B tools]\n\nAnswer three questions in complete sentences, using our step words. Under each box, the \"Your answer needs\" chips turn green as you include each part. Stuck? Press \"Need a hint?\" for one hint at a time. And \"My evidence\" adds anything you highlighted.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CAM 1 beach · CAM 2 woods]\n\nFor question five, pick an animal from last night's cameras. They're right above the question.","want":false},{"id":"partc","label":"Teacher · Part C · your own animal + sketch (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-partc","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"Now you're the scientist.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your scenario]\n\nMake up a short moment, like the heron story, about a Florida animal that wasn't in today's lesson. A pelican? A bobcat? A dolphin? Fill in the four boxes: what happened, what noticed it, what the brain decided, and what the animal did.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The sketch pad]\n\nIf you have time, sketch it too. It's a bonus. Draw with the pen. To add a label, pick Label, drag from a part of your drawing to an empty spot, and type. Add arrows to show the order.","want":true},{"id":"evidence","label":"Teacher · Use your evidence · CER (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-evidence","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The classmate says]\n\nA classmate says, \"Animals just react. They don't really need a brain. Their senses do all the work.\" Hmm. Think about the hidden pink box from the heron. Do you agree?\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nRemember CER: your claim, your evidence from today's lesson, and your reasoning.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The planner]\n\nTap \"I agree\" or \"I disagree,\" then tap two pieces of evidence. They drop right into your boxes! The reasoning is all you. Try starting with \"This shows that…\"","want":true},{"id":"closing","label":"Teacher · Case closed · finish strong (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-1-closing","done":true,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Case closed · Confetti]\n\nCase closed! You did it, scientists.\n\nFour animals noticed four different things with four different senses, and each one's brain decided what to do. The four steps solved it.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Before you go]\n\nTwo last things. Check every box in What to Turn In to earn a heron feather. And download your field journal.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes has a sneak peek at next time.","want":true}],"cards":{"sci":{"welcome":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"cam1","label":"📷 CAM 1 beach"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"cam2","label":"📷 CAM 2 woods"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"cam3","label":"📷 CAM 3 river"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"cam4","label":"📷 CAM 4 marsh"},{"label":"⏱ The times","tag":"NOTICE","title":"Look at the clock on each camera","col":"#B9822A","rows":[["9:14:08","CAM 1 · beach"],["9:14:31","CAM 2 · woods"],["9:15:02","CAM 3 · river"],["9:15:47","CAM 4 · marsh"]]},{"label":"🗂 The case question","tag":"CASE FILE 12","title":"The Night Everything Moved","col":"#B9822A","body":"What made four animals react at almost the same time, and how did each one know?"},{"label":"🧭 Your clue bar","tag":"HOW TO SOLVE IT","title":"Find 4 clues","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🗂 My clues","The bar under the tabs. Tap it to see your clues."],["? ? ? ?","Each game you finish fills in a clue."],["🔒","Crack the case at the top of the Assignment."]]}],"warmup":[{"label":"⏱ The snap test","tag":"WARM-UP · TRY IT FOR REAL","title":"Hear the snap","col":"#2F6FD6","rows":[["1","Tap the trail picture to start."],["2","Wait. Don’t guess!"],["3","The moment you hear SNAP, tap fast."],["3×","Try it three times."]]},{"label":"📝 Your steps","tag":"THEN WRITE","title":"What happened in your body?","col":"#2F6FD6","body":"Write at least three steps, in order. Start with the sound. End with you turning around."}],"vocab":[{"label":"🎭 Act it out","tag":"WORDS YOU’LL USE TODAY","title":"Act out the four steps","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["Stimulus","Snap your fingers."],["Sense organ","Point to your ear."],["Brain","Tap your head."],["Response","Turn around."]]},{"label":"🔭 Word Lens","tag":"HOW TO USE IT","title":"Tap a word sticker","col":"#7A5BB5","body":"Dr. Reyes reads the word and what it means. The words light up as she reads. Try to tap all six."}],"lesson":[{"label":"🗺 This tab","tag":"THE LESSON","title":"Four parts, three clues","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Part 1","The four steps, and the Send the Signal game (Clue 2)"],["Part 2","Different senses and the alligator picture"],["Part 3","Open last night’s four cameras (Clue 3)"],["Part 4","One more camera: sort the heron’s steps (Clue 4)"]]}],"part1":[{"label":"🔁 The four steps","tag":"THE FOUR-STEP PATHWAY","title":"Every animal reaction","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["⚡ Stimulus","Something happens in the world: a sound, a smell, a flash of light."],["👂 Sense organ","A body part picks it up: eyes, ears, nose, tongue or skin."],["🧠 Brain","The brain decides what it means."],["🏃 Response","The animal does something."]]},{"label":"📡 Send the signal","tag":"THE GAME BELOW","title":"Put the steps in order","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Drag","Drag each step onto a station, or tap a step, then a station."],["Start","Start where the message starts: out in the world."],["Watch","Fill all four and watch the signal run. That’s Clue 2!"]]}],"part2":[{"label":"👀 Five senses","tag":"PART 2","title":"Each sense organ picks up something different","col":"#16807B","rows":[["Eyes","light"],["Ears","sound and vibration (tiny, fast shaking)"],["Nose","smell"],["Tongue","taste"],["Skin","touch, heat and cold, and pain"]]},{"label":"⚠ Watch out","tag":"A COMMON MISTAKE","title":"The brain can’t do it alone","col":"#C8553D","body":"No sense organ, no signal. No signal, no response."}],"figure":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"pathway","label":"🐊 Alligator pathway"},{"label":"➡ Read it left to right","tag":"READ THE PICTURE","title":"Left to right","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Splash","the stimulus"],["Ear","the sense organ"],["Brain","decides"],["Turn","the response"]]}],"part3":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"cam1","label":"📷 CAM 1 beach"},{"label":"📹 How to open a camera","tag":"LAST NIGHT’S TRAIL CAMERAS","title":"Open all four","col":"#B9822A","rows":[["Tap","Tap a camera to open it."],["Next step","Press Next step to follow the message one step at a time."],["Ask","What started this reaction?"]]},{"kind":"photo","pic":"manatee","label":"🦭 The manatee photo"}],"explore":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"heron","label":"🪶 The heron"},{"label":"📖 The heron story","tag":"CAM 5 · 9:16 PM","title":"A heron hunting","col":"#B9822A","body":"A great blue heron stands still in shallow water. A small fish swims into view. The heron freezes, then strikes. Its sharp beak grabs the fish."},{"label":"🃏 One card, sorted","tag":"WATCH ME SORT ONE","title":"A small fish swims into view.","col":"#B9822A","rows":[["Stimulus","It happens out in the water, before the heron does anything. It starts everything."]]},{"label":"🧠 The hidden step","tag":"THE PINK BOX","title":"No camera can see it","col":"#C8553D","body":"The brain step happens inside the heron. Keep it in mind: it’s great evidence for your CER."}],"quiz":[{"label":"🧠 Self-Check","tag":"NOT GRADED","title":"Just for you","col":"#2F6FD6","rows":[["One at a time","Answer, then press Next question."],["Oops?","Read the hint. It tells you what to think about."],["Score","See your score at the end."]]}],"assignment":[{"label":"🔎 Crack the case","tag":"CRACK THE CASE","title":"What happened that night?","col":"#B9822A","rows":[["🔒","Locked until you have all 4 clues."],["🔎","Missing one? Tap “Show me where the clues are.”"],["✔","Pick the answer that fits all four clues."]]},{"label":"📋 Today’s parts","tag":"YOUR ASSIGNMENT","title":"Three parts","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Part B","Answer 2 questions in complete sentences."],["Part C","Make up a Florida animal moment. A sketch is a bonus."],["CER","Agree or disagree with a classmate, using evidence."]]},{"label":"💾 Saved for you","tag":"TURNING IT IN","title":"Everything is gathered at the bottom","col":"#2F6FD6","body":"Every answer you type saves itself and shows up in Gathered Answers at the bottom of this tab."}],"partb":[{"label":"✍ Part B tools","tag":"PART B","title":"Help is built in","col":"#2F6FD6","rows":[["Your answer needs","The chips light up green as you include each part."],["💡 Need a hint?","One hint at a time."],["📎 My evidence","Use your highlights or photos as evidence."]]},{"kind":"photo","pic":"cam1","label":"📷 CAM 1 beach"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"cam2","label":"📷 CAM 2 woods"}],"partc":[{"label":"🐾 Your scenario","tag":"PART C","title":"A made-up moment, like the heron story","col":"#B9822A","rows":[["Animal","A Florida animal NOT in today’s lesson."],["4 boxes","Stimulus, sense organ, brain, response."],["Ideas","Pelican, bobcat, anole lizard, armadillo, dolphin…"]]},{"label":"✏ The sketch pad","tag":"BONUS · SKETCH IT","title":"Draw, then label","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["✏️ Pen","Draw your animal and what it noticed."],["🏷️ Label","Drag from a part of your drawing to an empty spot, then type."],["Arrows","Show the order of the four steps."]]}],"evidence":[{"label":"💬 The classmate says","tag":"USE YOUR EVIDENCE","title":"A classmate says…","col":"#C8553D","body":"“Animals just react. They don’t really need a brain. Their senses do all the work.”"},{"label":"⚖ CER","tag":"REMEMBER CER?","title":"Claim · Evidence · Reasoning","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["C","Claim: what you think is true."],["E","Evidence: facts from the lesson that prove it."],["R","Reasoning: how the facts prove your claim."]]},{"label":"🧩 The planner","tag":"PLAN BEFORE YOU WRITE","title":"Tap to plan","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["1","Tap I agree or I disagree."],["2","Tap two pieces of evidence."],["3","They drop into your C and E boxes. You write the R."]]}],"closing":[{"label":"🎉 Case closed","tag":"CASE CLOSED","title":"Four animals, four pathways","col":"#2F7A55","body":"Each animal noticed its own stimulus with its own sense organ, and its own brain decided what to do."},{"label":"✅ Before you go","tag":"FINISH STRONG","title":"Two last things","col":"#2F6FD6","rows":[["☑","Check off the boxes in What to Turn In."],["📓","Download your field journal, with your photos and sketch."]]},{"label":"🔜 Next time","tag":"NEXT LESSON","title":"Two animals, side by side","col":"#2F6FD6","body":"Build models of two Florida animals’ pathways and compare them."}]}},"words":[{"w":"Stimulus","d":"Something in the world that an animal can notice — a sound, a smell, a flash of light, a touch.","e":"⚡"},{"w":"Sense organ","d":"A body part that picks up information from the world. Eyes, ears, noses, tongues, and skin are all sense organs.","e":"👂"},{"w":"Brain","d":"The part of an animal that takes in information from the senses, decides what it means, and tells the body what to do.","e":"🧠"},{"w":"Response","d":"What an animal does after its brain decides — running, freezing, jumping, eating, hiding, calling out.","e":"🏃"},{"w":"Signal","d":"A tiny message that travels from a sense organ to the brain. Signals carry the information your senses pick up.","e":"📡"},{"w":"Information","d":"Something an animal learns from the world — like the smell of an animal that might eat it, the sight of food, or the sound of a splash.","e":"📨"}]},{"w":12,"d":"02","title":"Lesson 12.02: Model: From Senses to Brain","file":"lesson-12-02-model-senses-to-brain.html","fileUrl":"../../optima-4th-science/lesson-12-02-model-senses-to-brain.html","clips":[{"id":"welcome","label":"Teacher · Welcome · the silent camera (50–60 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-2-welcome","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Welcome back, scientists! The case isn’t over. Dr. Reyes put one more camera by the pond.\n\n[ON SCREEN: 9:41 PM · 9:52 PM]\n\nAt 9:41, moths are flying around the porch light. Eleven minutes later, they’re gone, and the camera saw nothing move. Hmm.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The printout]\n\nBut her bat detector heard something at that exact minute. Look: slow clicks, faster clicks, then a buzz. I wonder what that means.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nThat’s our case. Find four clues today. Press Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie, right below me.","want":true},{"id":"warmup","label":"Teacher · Warm-Up · echo hunt (Clue 1) (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-2-warmup","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"(Clap once, then pretend to listen.) If I clap near a wall, the sound bounces back. That’s an echo.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Echo]\n\nBats use echoes to hunt. A bat clicks, and the echo off a moth comes back to its ears.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Test click]\n\nIn the echo hunt, press Click and read how long the echo took. A quicker echo means a closer moth. Use the test click to help you, then drag the pin to the moth. Find it three times.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes first.","want":true},{"id":"vocab","label":"Teacher · Word Wall (30–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-2-vocab","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Pathway]\n\nA pathway is the path a message follows: stimulus, sense organ, brain, response. (Trace four steps in the air.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Model]\n\nA model shows how something works. Today you build pathway models.\n\nTap each word below me to hear it.","want":false},{"id":"part1","label":"Teacher · Part 1 · build a strong model (Clue 2) (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-2-part1","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The bonneted bat]\n\nMeet our suspect: the Florida bonneted bat, Florida’s biggest bat. Look at those ears!\n\n[ON SCREEN: A strong model]\n\nA strong model has four things: the four steps in order, an arrow between each step, the word signal on the arrow to the brain, and a caption about a survival need.\n\nBuild one for the bat in the game below. Watch the meter fill up as your model gets stronger.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":true},{"id":"figure","label":"Teacher · The eagle and bat picture (25–35 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-2-figure","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Two pathways]\n\nRead this picture like two sentences. Top row, the eagle: a fish flashes, the eye sees it, a signal goes to the brain, the eagle dives. Bottom row, the bat. Point to each step. Which step looks the most different?","want":false},{"id":"part3","label":"Teacher · Part 3 · match the sense (Clue 3) (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-2-part3","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Match]\n\nFour Florida animals, four different senses. Read what each one notices, then drag in the sense organ that can pick it up. When all four are done, look across. What stayed the same? Press Listen first.","want":false},{"id":"explore","label":"Teacher · Part 4 · fix the broken models (Clue 4) (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-2-explore","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Broken model]\n\nOne of the volunteers drew this. An echo bounces off a moth, and the bat grabs it. (Count on your fingers: one, two.) Hmm, only two steps. I notice something’s missing.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Run it]\n\nIn Part 4, pick what’s wrong, drag in the missing step, then press Run the model and watch the signal go all the way through. There are three broken models: the bat, the eagle and the bear.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":true},{"id":"quiz","label":"Teacher · Self-Check (15–20 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-2-quiz","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Quick check: three questions, one at a time. It’s not graded. If you miss one, read the hint and try again.","want":false},{"id":"assignment","label":"Teacher · Assignment · crack the case (45–55 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-2-assignment","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nYou made it to the assignment! First, crack the case at the top. Still locked? Tap “Show me where the clues are.” Pick the answer that fits every clue.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your case report]\n\nThen three parts. Part B: two short answers. Part C: build two pathway models for two Florida animals that use different senses, then compare them. A sketch is a bonus. And last, your CER.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":true},{"id":"partc","label":"Teacher · Part C · two pathway models (25–35 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-2-partc","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Two models]\n\nPick two Florida animals that use different senses, not the bat or the eagle. Maybe an owl that hears and a snake that smells? Write the four steps for each, then tell how they’re alike and different.","want":false},{"id":"evidence","label":"Teacher · Use your evidence · CER (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-2-evidence","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The quote]\n\nA classmate says one model is enough to understand every animal. Hmm.\n\nThink back. Would a bat model tell you how a bear finds berries? Or how a crab feels the water?\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nPick your side in the planner, tap two pieces of evidence, then write your reasoning in your own words. Try starting with “This shows that…”\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes.","want":true},{"id":"closing","label":"Teacher · Case closed (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-2-closing","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Confetti]\n\nCase closed! A bat clicked, its ears caught the echoes, and its brain worked out where the moths were. Same four steps as the eagle, just a different sense.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your specimen]\n\nCheck every box in What to Turn In, and the bat detector printout goes into your Field Notebook.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next week]\n\nNext week: families! Why do baby animals look like their parents?\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s last word.","want":true},{"id":"lesson","label":"Teacher · The Lesson (25–35 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-2-lesson","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"part2","label":"Teacher · Part 2 · two animals, two pathways (20–30 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-2-part2","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"partb","label":"Teacher · Part B (30 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-12-2-partb","done":false,"script":""}],"cards":{"sci":{"welcome":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"moth","label":"📷 9:41 PM"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"empty","label":"📷 9:52 PM"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"detector","label":"🎙 The printout"},{"label":"🗂 The case question","tag":"CASE FILE 12 · PART 2","title":"The Silent Camera","col":"#B9822A","body":"What caught the moths in the dark, and how did it find them?"}],"warmup":[{"label":"🔊 Echo","tag":"WARM-UP GAME","title":"What’s an echo?","col":"#2F7A55","body":"A sound that bounces off something and comes back."},{"label":"📏 Test click","tag":"USE THIS","title":"The test click","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["3 m away","17 ms"],["Quicker echo","closer moth"],["Slower echo","farther moth"]]}],"vocab":[],"part1":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"bat","label":"📷 The bonneted bat"},{"label":"✅ A strong model","tag":"PART 1","title":"What makes a model strong","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["1","Four steps, in order"],["2","An arrow between each step"],["3","“Signal” on the arrow to the brain"],["4","A caption about a survival need"]]}],"figure":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"pathways","label":"📷 Two pathways"}],"part3":[{"label":"👂 Match","tag":"PART 3 GAME","title":"Match the sense","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🔊 Sound","ears"],["💡 Light","eyes"],["🌸 Smell","nose"],["〰️ Touch","feelers"]]}],"explore":[{"label":"🛠 Broken model","tag":"PART 4 GAME","title":"A volunteer drew:","col":"#C8553D","body":"An echo bounces off a moth → The bat grabs the moth"},{"label":"▶ Run it","tag":"HOW TO FIX IT","title":"Fix, then run","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["1","Pick what’s wrong"],["2","Drag in the missing step"],["3","Press Run the model"]]}],"quiz":[],"assignment":[{"label":"🔓 Crack the case","tag":"TOP OF THE ASSIGNMENT","title":"What happened at the pond?","col":"#B9822A","body":"Pick the answer that fits all four clues."},{"label":"✍️ Your case report","tag":"WHAT TO WRITE","title":"Three parts","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Part B","Two short answers"],["Part C","Two pathway models + compare them"],["CER","Claim, evidence, reasoning"]]}],"partc":[{"label":"✏️ Two models","tag":"PART C","title":"Build two, then compare","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["1️⃣","Animal 1: four steps"],["2️⃣","Animal 2: four steps"],["🟰","Alike because…"],["↔️","Different because…"]]}],"evidence":[{"label":"💬 The quote","tag":"USE YOUR EVIDENCE","title":"A classmate says…","col":"#C8553D","body":"“You only need to build one pathway model to understand how all animals respond. Building two is a waste of time.”"},{"label":"⚖ CER","tag":"REMEMBER CER?","title":"Claim · Evidence · Reasoning","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["C","Claim: what you think is true."],["E","Evidence: facts from the lesson that prove it."],["R","Reasoning: how the facts prove your claim."]]}],"closing":[{"label":"🦇 Your specimen","tag":"FIELD NOTEBOOK","title":"Bat detector printout","col":"#B9822A","body":"Finish the checklist to stamp CASE CLOSED."},{"label":"➡ Next week","tag":"LESSON 13.01","title":"Like parent, like child","col":"#2F7A55","body":"How do Florida plants and animals pass their traits to their young?"}]}},"words":[{"w":"Stimulus","d":"Something in the world that an animal can notice — a sound, a smell, a flash of light, a touch.","e":"⚡"},{"w":"Pathway","d":"A path with steps that information follows — from the world, through the body, to a response.","e":"➡️"},{"w":"Process","d":"What the brain does — takes a signal in and figures out what it means before sending an action message back.","e":"⚙️"},{"w":"Response","d":"What an animal does after its brain decides — running, freezing, jumping, eating, hiding, calling out.","e":"🏃"},{"w":"Model","d":"A drawing, diagram, build, or explanation that shows how something works — like a pathway from stimulus to response.","e":"🧩"},{"w":"Scenario","d":"A short story of one moment — a situation where an animal notices something and reacts. Scientists use scenarios to test ideas about pathways.","e":"🎬"}]},{"w":13,"d":"01","title":"Lesson 13.01: Like Parent, Like Child","file":"lesson-13-01-like-parent-like-child.html","fileUrl":"../../optima-4th-science/lesson-13-01-like-parent-like-child.html","clips":[{"id":"welcome","label":"Teacher · Welcome · one parent, two gardens (50–60 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-13-1-welcome","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Welcome, scientists! New week, new case.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The parent]\n\nThis is a wild beach sunflower on a Florida dune. Dr. Reyes collected its seeds.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Pot A · Pot B]\n\nShe planted some in the sun and some in the shade. Look how different they are! One is bushy with tons of flowers. One is tall and skinny. Hmm. I wonder… did the seeds get mixed up?\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nThat’s our case. Find four clues today. Press Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie, right below me.","want":true},{"id":"warmup","label":"Teacher · Warm-Up · find the family (Clue 1) (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-13-1-warmup","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Baby]\n\nLook at this baby. Spots! Blue eyes!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Parent]\n\nNow look at its mom: plain tan, no spots. Same face, same round ears, though. This kitten is a Florida panther, and its spots fade as it grows.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Look for]\n\nIn the warm-up game, match four Florida babies to their parents. Look at body shape first, because colors can fool you. Then list the traits the baby inherited.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes first.","want":true},{"id":"vocab","label":"Teacher · Word Wall (30–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-13-1-vocab","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Inherited]\n\nInherited means passed down from your parents. (Point from yourself back over your shoulder.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Environment]\n\nEnvironment is where you live and grow. (Spread your arms wide.)\n\nTap each word below me to hear it.","want":false},{"id":"part2","label":"Teacher · Part 2 · grow the twins (Clue 2) (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-13-1-part2","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"(Hold up two fingers.) Two seeds. Same parent.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two pots]\n\nIn Grow the twins, you pick the garden for each pot: sun or shade, lots of water or a little. Then press Grow.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Watch for]\n\nYour job is to notice. Which traits change when the garden changes? And which ones never change, no matter what? Make sure one pot grows in the shade.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":true},{"id":"part3","label":"Teacher · Part 3 · stamp the case (Clue 3) (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-13-1-part3","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Three stamps]\n\nFour Florida cases, three stamps. Read the facts, stamp each one, then pick the fact that proves it. Watch out: two of them need the BOTH stamp.","want":false},{"id":"explore","label":"Teacher · Part 4 · pass it on? (Clue 4) (40–50 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-13-1-explore","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The mother]\n\nThis panther mom was caught on a trail camera, carrying her kitten. She just had a litter.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Guess]\n\nShe has a kinked tail. A scar on her nose. Round ears. And she’s thin after a hard year. (Tap your chin.) Which of those will her kittens get?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next generation]\n\nGuess for each trait, then press Next generation. A wrong guess is fine. That’s how scientists learn.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":true},{"id":"quiz","label":"Teacher · Self-Check (15–20 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-13-1-quiz","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"Quick check: three questions, one at a time. It’s not graded. If you miss one, read the hint and try again.","want":false},{"id":"assignment","label":"Teacher · Assignment · crack the case (45–55 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-13-1-assignment","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nYou made it! First, crack the case at the top. Still locked? Tap “Show me where the clues are.” Pick the answer that fits every clue.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your case report]\n\nThen three parts. Part B: two short answers. Part C: pick a real parent and its offspring, maybe your pet or a backyard plant. Name three inherited traits and two from the environment. A sketch is a bonus. Last, your CER.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.","want":true},{"id":"partc","label":"Teacher · Part C · parent and offspring (25–35 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-13-1-partc","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Your pair]\n\nPick a real parent and offspring. A dog and her puppy? A mango tree and its seedling? Name three inherited traits and two from the environment, then tell how you can tell the difference.","want":false},{"id":"evidence","label":"Teacher · Use your evidence · CER (35–45 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-13-1-evidence","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: The quote]\n\nA classmate says babies are clones of their parents. A clone is an exact copy. Hmm.\n\nThink back. Were the two sunflower pots exact copies? Did the kittens look exactly like their mom?\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nPick your side in the planner, tap two pieces of evidence, then write your reasoning in your own words. Try starting with “This shows that…”\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes.","want":true},{"id":"closing","label":"Teacher · Case closed (30–40 s)","path":"teacher/g4sci-13-1-closing","done":false,"redo":false,"script":"[ON SCREEN: Confetti]\n\nCase closed! Same parent, different gardens. The flower color and the leaves came from the parent. The sun did the rest.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your specimen]\n\nCheck every box in What to Turn In, and a seed packet goes into your Field Notebook.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next lesson]\n\nNext time: how did one flower make all those seeds?\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s last word.","want":true},{"id":"lesson","label":"Teacher · The Lesson (25–35 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-13-1-lesson","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"part1","label":"Teacher · Part 1 · traits are passed down (20–30 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-13-1-part1","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"partb","label":"Teacher · Part B (30 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-13-1-partb","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"figure","label":"Teacher · The inherited or environmental chart (20–30 s) · optional, no script yet","path":"teacher/g4sci-13-1-figure","done":false,"script":""}],"cards":{"sci":{"welcome":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"parent","label":"🌻 The parent"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"sun","label":"☀️ Pot A"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"shade","label":"🌳 Pot B"},{"label":"🗂 The case question","tag":"CASE FILE 13","title":"One Parent, Two Gardens","col":"#B9822A","body":"Did the seeds get mixed up, or can one parent plant have offspring this different?"}],"warmup":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"kitten","label":"🐾 Baby"},{"kind":"photo","pic":"panther","label":"🐆 Parent"},{"label":"🔎 Look for","tag":"WARM-UP GAME","title":"Find the family","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["👀","Body shape first"],["🎨","Colors can fool you"],["✍️","Then list the inherited traits"]]}],"vocab":[],"part2":[{"label":"🌱 Two pots","tag":"PART 2 GAME","title":"Grow the twins","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Pot A","one seed from the parent"],["Pot B","one seed from the SAME parent"],["You pick","sun or shade, lots of water or a little"]]},{"label":"🔎 Watch for","tag":"YOUR JOB","title":"What changes? What never changes?","col":"#7A5BB5","body":"Height · spread · flowers · flower color · leaf shape"}],"part3":[{"label":"🗂 Three stamps","tag":"PART 3 GAME","title":"Stamp the case","col":"#B9822A","rows":[["👪","INHERITED: from the parents"],["🌍","ENVIRONMENT: from where it lives"],["⚖️","BOTH: a little of each"]]}],"explore":[{"kind":"photo","pic":"mom","label":"🐆 The mother"},{"label":"🤔 Guess","tag":"PART 4 GAME","title":"Will the kittens get it?","col":"#C8553D","rows":[["〰️","A kinked tail"],["🩹","A scar on her nose"],["👂","Round ears"],["🦌","Thin after a hard year"]]},{"kind":"photo","pic":"kits","label":"🐾 Next generation"}],"quiz":[],"assignment":[{"label":"🔓 Crack the case","tag":"TOP OF THE ASSIGNMENT","title":"What happened in the two gardens?","col":"#B9822A","body":"Pick the answer that fits all four clues."},{"label":"✍️ Your case report","tag":"WHAT TO WRITE","title":"Three parts","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["Part B","Two short answers"],["Part C","A parent and its offspring: 3 inherited + 2 environment traits"],["CER","Claim, evidence, reasoning"]]}],"partc":[{"label":"👪 Your pair","tag":"PART C","title":"A parent and its offspring","col":"#2F7A55","rows":[["🧬","Three inherited traits"],["🌍","Two traits from the environment"],["🔎","How you can tell"]]}],"evidence":[{"label":"💬 The quote","tag":"USE YOUR EVIDENCE","title":"A classmate says…","col":"#C8553D","body":"“Offspring are basically clones of their parents. Everything they look like comes from inheritance.”"},{"label":"⚖ CER","tag":"REMEMBER CER?","title":"Claim · Evidence · Reasoning","col":"#7A5BB5","rows":[["C","Claim: what you think is true."],["E","Evidence: facts from the lesson that prove it."],["R","Reasoning: how the facts prove your claim."]]}],"closing":[{"label":"🌻 Your specimen","tag":"FIELD NOTEBOOK","title":"Beach sunflower seed packet","col":"#B9822A","body":"Finish the checklist to stamp CASE CLOSED."},{"label":"➡ Next lesson","tag":"LESSON 13.02","title":"Where do seeds come from?","col":"#2F7A55","body":"How did one beach sunflower make all those seeds?"}]}},"words":[{"w":"Trait","d":"A feature of a living thing — eye color, beak shape, fur length, leaf shape. Some come from parents; some come from where you grew up.","e":"🧬"},{"w":"Inherited","d":"A trait passed down from parents to offspring. You inherit traits — you don't choose them or earn them.","e":"👪"},{"w":"Environment","d":"Where a living thing lives and grows. The environment can change traits like SIZE, scars, fur color, or how strong a tree grows.","e":"🌍"},{"w":"Offspring","d":"A young plant or animal — a chick, puppy, calf, sapling, or seedling. Offspring inherit traits from their parents.","e":"🐣"},{"w":"Resemble","d":"To look or be similar to. Offspring resemble their parents because they inherit many of the same traits.","e":"👀"},{"w":"Variation","d":"Small differences between offspring and their parents — even though traits are inherited, offspring are never EXACTLY the same as their parents.","e":"🧒"}]}],"poems":[],"teacher":[],"teacherDocs":{"10-01":"# Lesson 10.01 teacher videos\n\n## Welcome · open the case · g4sci-10-1-welcome\n\nHi, scientists! Welcome back to the Field Station. Today’s case starts with a tree.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Before the oak · Before the owl]\n\nThis giant live oak stood in a Florida hammock, a shady patch of forest. A barred owl lived up in its limbs.\n\n[ON SCREEN: After the fallen oak · After the mushrooms]\n\nThen a storm knocked it over. Look what showed up on the trunk. Hmm. Only ONE tree fell, but the owl left, the squirrels left, and mushrooms moved in.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nThat’s our case: why did so much change?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your clue bar]\n\nEvery game you finish adds a clue to “My clues.” Press Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie, right below me.\n\n## Warm-Up · tie the threads (Clue 1) · g4sci-10-1-warmup\n\nLet’s meet the oak’s neighbors.\n\n(Hold up a piece of string.) Scientists draw a line, or a thread, for every connection between living things.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The five threads]\n\nHere are five kinds of threads. Only one is about eating!\n\n[ON SCREEN: How to tie]\n\nTap a visitor to read what it does with the oak. Then drag the thread that fits, or tap the thread and then the visitor. One visitor isn’t even alive. Can you spot it?\n\nWhen all five are tied, list what you found in the boxes. Press Listen. Dr. Reyes has a tip for you.\n\n## Part 3 · pull a thread (Clue 3) · g4sci-10-1-part3\n\n(Hold a string web or a cat’s cradle, then tug one string.) Watch. I pull one string, and look how many others move!\n\n[ON SCREEN: The web]\n\nThis is the web around Dr. Reyes’s oak. Each colored line is one connection.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Predict first]\n\nPick a thread to pull. Before you pull, predict: tap every living thing you think will change. Then press Pull the thread and see how close you were. Try all three.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie. One of these pulls surprised Dr. Reyes!\n\n## Part 4 · the saw palmetto (Clue 4) · g4sci-10-1-explore\n\n[ON SCREEN: Saw palmetto]\n\nHave you seen this plant? Saw palmetto grows all over Florida. Its stems have tiny teeth, like a saw. Ouch!\n\nIt’s short, but it has a web of its own.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Five boxes]\n\nRead about it, then drag each card into its kind of connection. One card goes in each box. That’s your last clue!\n\nPress Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie first.\n\n## Assignment · crack the case · g4sci-10-1-assignment\n\n[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nYou found all four clues! At the top of this tab, crack the case: why did so much change when only one tree fell?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your case report]\n\nThen write your case report: two short answers, your own ecosystem web, and a CER.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Help buttons]\n\nWatch the chips light up as you write. Stuck? Press Hint. Your answers save as you type.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Use your evidence · CER · g4sci-10-1-evidence\n\n[ON SCREEN: The quote]\n\nSome people say, “As long as we have plants and animals, the small stuff doesn’t matter.” Bees. Mushrooms. Bacteria. Hmm. Think about the threads you pulled.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nRemember CER: your claim, your evidence from today, and your reasoning.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The planner]\n\nTap your side, then two pieces of evidence. They drop right into your boxes. The reasoning is all you. Try starting with “This shows that…”\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes wants to hear your side.\n\n## Case closed · g4sci-10-1-closing\n\nCase closed! One tree was the center of a whole web.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your specimen]\n\nFinish your checklist and a live oak acorn goes into your Field Notebook.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Confetti]\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nNext time, we’ll look at real numbers from Florida. How can you tell if a place is healthy, or out of balance?\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s last word.\n\n## Part 1 · the real live oak (Clue 2) · g4sci-10-1-part1\n\n[ON SCREEN: The live oak photo]\n\nThis is a real live oak, the same kind as Dr. Reyes’s tree. See those little ferns on the limbs? Open every number, then match each kind of connection to a number. That’s Clue 2! Press Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Self-Check · g4sci-10-1-quiz\n\nQuick check: three questions, one at a time. It’s not graded. If you miss one, read the hint and try again.\n\n## Part C · your own ecosystem web · g4sci-10-1-partc\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your place]\n\nNow it’s your web. Pick a Florida place you know. Name five living things there, then three connections, each a different kind. Want to draw it? The sketch pad is a bonus.\n","10-02":"# Lesson 10.02 teacher videos\n\n## Welcome · the one-year jump · g4sci-10-2-welcome\n\nHi, scientists! Last time, one fallen oak changed a whole forest. Today: how do scientists know if a whole ecosystem is healthy?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Manatees from the air]\n\nEvery winter, scientists fly over Florida in small planes and count manatees. Those gray shapes? Manatees!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two flight logs]\n\nLook at these two flight logs. 2009: 3,802. One year later: 5,077. That’s more than twelve hundred extra manatees in one year. Hmm. Manatees usually have one baby every few years.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nSo did that many really show up? Find the four clues. Press Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie, right below me.\n\n## Warm-Up · count it or feel it? (Clue 1) · g4sci-10-2-warmup\n\n(Step outside or look out a window.) Is it a nice day? I think so. But you might not! That’s a feeling.\n\nHow many clouds are in the sky? That we can count.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two sides]\n\nScientists trust things they can count or measure, not feelings.\n\n[ON SCREEN: How to play]\n\nRead each card about two Florida wetlands. Drag it to the side it belongs on, or tap the side. Then list three things a scientist could measure. Press Listen. Dr. Reyes has a field secret for you.\n\n## Part 2 · fly the survey (Clue 2) · g4sci-10-2-part2\n\n[ON SCREEN: Manatees in a warm spring]\n\nWhen the water gets cold, manatees crowd into warm springs like this one. Easy to count! On warm days they spread out all over.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The practice flight]\n\nNow you fly. Two hundred manatees live near this spring, every single day. Pick the weather and the wind, then fly. Write down your count. Now change just one thing and fly again.\n\nDid the manatees change, or did your count? That’s Clue 2. Press Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Part 4 · thirty years of counts (Clue 4) · g4sci-10-2-explore\n\n(Hold up one sock.) If I find one sock on the floor, is my room messy? Maybe! I need more evidence.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two dots]\n\nThis chart starts with just two survey flights, one year apart. Up or down? Decide.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Every flight]\n\nThen press Show every flight. Thirty years of real dots! Some years jump, some dip. The dashed line shows the trend, the way the numbers move over many years.\n\nThat’s your last clue. Press Listen.\n\n## Assignment · crack the case · g4sci-10-2-assignment\n\n[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nYou found all four clues! Crack the case at the top of this tab. Did twelve hundred manatees really appear in one year?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your case report]\n\nThen your case report: two short answers, a graph, and a CER.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Part C graph]\n\nIn Part C, you build the graph. Tap in each column to set the dot at the right height. Then write one sentence: which way did the numbers go?\n\nStuck? Press Hint under any box. Press Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Use your evidence · CER · g4sci-10-2-evidence\n\n[ON SCREEN: A Burmese python]\n\nThis snake is not from Florida. It came from Asia.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The quote]\n\nSome people say invasive species are no big deal, because Florida has plenty of native animals. Hmm. Do you agree?\n\n[ON SCREEN: The planner]\n\nRemember CER. Tap your side, then two facts from the lesson. Numbers make strong evidence! Then explain how your facts prove your claim. Try starting with “This shows that…”\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes wants to hear your side.\n\n## Case closed · g4sci-10-2-closing\n\nCase closed! One count can fool you. Many years of counts tell the truth.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your specimen]\n\nFinish your checklist and a Florida apple snail shell goes into your Field Notebook.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Confetti]\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nNext time, we zoom in. Why does a spoonbill have a flat, spoon-shaped beak? We’ll find out how body parts help living things survive.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s last word.\n\n## Part 3 · four Florida stories (Clue 3) · g4sci-10-2-part3\n\n[ON SCREEN: A Florida manatee]\n\nFour Florida stories, four case files. Read each one, stamp it Recovering or Out of balance, then find the line that proves it. Careful: some lines are true but don’t prove anything. Press Listen.\n\n## Self-Check · g4sci-10-2-quiz\n\nQuick check: three questions, one at a time. It’s not graded. If you miss one, read the hint and try again.\n","11-01":"# Lesson 11.01 teacher videos\n\n## Welcome · the washed-out notebook · g4sci-11-1-welcome\n\nHi, scientists! Today’s case starts with a soggy notebook.\n\n(Hold up a notebook.) Dr. Reyes found one like this on a boardwalk, soaked by the rain. Only four photos survived.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The beak · Beak in the water]\n\nLook at that beak. It’s flat, like a spoon!\n\n[ON SCREEN: The legs · Where I found it]\n\nLong pink legs, standing in shallow water.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nThe last line is washed out. This bird finds food it can’t even see… how?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your clue bar]\n\nEvery game you finish adds a clue. Press Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie, right below me.\n\n## Warm-Up · beak or tool (Clue 1) · g4sci-11-1-warmup\n\n(Hold up a spoon and a straw.) Which one would you use to scoop soup? Which one to sip a drink? The shape tells you the job!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Four tools]\n\nBird beaks work the same way. Here are four tools.\n\n[ON SCREEN: How to match]\n\nLook at each beak’s shape. Drag the tool with the same shape onto it, or tap the tool and then the beak. Then write your best guess: why is a flat beak good for sweeping?\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes has a tip for you.\n\n## Part 2 · inside or outside (Clue 2) · g4sci-11-1-part2\n\n(Point to your skin, then to your heart.) My skin is external. I can see it. My heart is internal. It’s hidden inside.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two sides]\n\nSort each card. Ask: could I see this part from the outside? Watch the last card. It’s a clue! Press Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Part 3 · how deep can you go (Clue 3) · g4sci-11-1-part3\n\n(Hold a ruler up to your leg.) If I stood in water this deep, my knees would stay dry. Up to here? I’d be swimming!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Four depths]\n\nTap a water depth and watch the water rise.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Three birds]\n\nThree birds, three kinds of legs. Who can still stand and feed? Try every depth, then answer the question.\n\nPress Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie first.\n\n## Part 4 · build a hiding head (Clue 4) · g4sci-11-1-explore\n\n(Cover your mouth and nose with your hand.) Can you breathe? Not easily!\n\n[ON SCREEN: A gator hiding]\n\nNow look at this alligator. Its whole body is hiding underwater. What’s still sticking out?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Build and test]\n\nBuild a model head. Put the eyes and nostrils where you want, then sink it. Can it watch? Can it breathe? Test at least two designs. That’s your last clue!\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Self-Check · g4sci-11-1-quiz\n\nQuick check: three questions, one at a time. It’s not graded. If you miss one, read the hint and try again.\n\n## Assignment · crack the case · g4sci-11-1-assignment\n\n[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nYou found all four clues! At the top of this tab, crack the case: how does the mystery bird find food it can’t see?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your case report]\n\nThen write your case report: two short answers, a Florida plant or animal with three of its structures, and a CER.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Help buttons]\n\nWatch the chips light up as you write. Stuck? Press Hint. Your answers save as you type.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Part C · label a Florida organism · g4sci-11-1-partc\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your pick]\n\nNow it’s your turn. Pick a Florida plant or animal, but not the spoonbill. Name three of its structures, the job of each one, and whether it’s internal or external. Want to draw it? The sketch pad is a bonus.\n\n## Use your evidence · CER · g4sci-11-1-evidence\n\n[ON SCREEN: The quote]\n\nA classmate says plants don’t really have structures. Only animals have body parts that matter. Hmm!\n\n(Hold up a leaf or a plant.) What does this leaf do? What about the roots underneath?\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nRemember CER? Claim, evidence, reasoning.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The planner]\n\nTap your side, tap two pieces of evidence, then write your reasoning in your own words.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes.\n\n## Case closed · g4sci-11-1-closing\n\nCase closed! The spoonbill finds food by feeling, not seeing. Every part fits its job.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your specimen]\n\nFinish your checklist and a pink spoonbill feather goes into your Field Notebook.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Confetti]\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nNext time, you’ll build a model, a drawing that explains how a body part does its job. Bring your clues!\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s last word.\n","11-02":"# Lesson 11.02 teacher videos\n\n## Welcome · the refuge sign · g4sci-11-2-welcome\n\nWelcome back, scientists! Remember the soggy notebook? Our case report made it to the refuge, and now they want a sign about the spoonbill.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Draft A · Draft B · Draft C]\n\nThree people sent drafts. A photo with labels. A beautiful painting from 1836. And a photo with arrows.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nOnly one can go on the sign. Which one really explains how the spoonbill survives?\n\nFind four clues today. Press Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie, right below me.\n\n## Warm-Up · point the arrow (Clue 1) · g4sci-11-2-warmup\n\n(Hold up a sticky note that says “teeth.”) If I stick this on an alligator drawing, what do you learn? Just a name.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The teeth]\n\nNow watch. (Draw an arrow in the air toward a pretend fish.) An arrow points from the part to what it acts on.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two drawings]\n\nBuild Drawing 2: drag the arrow, then pick the action words. Then tell me which drawing teaches a friend more.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s field trick.\n\n## Part 2 · upgrade the drawing (Clue 2) · g4sci-11-2-part2\n\n[ON SCREEN: The anhinga model]\n\nLook at this anhinga. Every arrow goes from a part to what it does. The words at the bottom say why it matters.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Three steps]\n\nNow you upgrade a spoonbill photo, one step at a time: a label, an arrow, a caption. Watch the meter fill up as your drawing turns into a model.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Part 3 · chain it (Clue 3) · g4sci-11-2-part3\n\n[ON SCREEN: The chain]\n\nEvery strong model follows a chain: the part, what it does, and the need it meets. Build four chains. Careful: two cards in each set are tricks. Press Listen first.\n\n## Part 4 · the model kit (Clue 4) · g4sci-11-2-explore\n\n(Hold up a photo or a picture from a book.) Scientists don’t always draw from scratch. Sometimes they draw arrows right on a photo!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Model kit]\n\nPick a Florida organism: a tree frog, a gopher tortoise, an anhinga or a manatee. Tap a part, point the arrow, and pick what it does and which need it meets. Your caption writes itself. Build two if you can!\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Self-Check · g4sci-11-2-quiz\n\nQuick check: three questions, one at a time. It’s not graded. If you miss one, read the hint and try again.\n\n## Assignment · crack the case · g4sci-11-2-assignment\n\n[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nYou found all four clues! At the top of this tab, crack the case: which draft goes on the refuge sign, and what would fix the other two?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your case report]\n\nThen write your case report. In Part C, you build your own model in words: a label, an arrow, a caption. Watch the chips light up. Stuck? Press Hint.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Part C · build an annotated model · g4sci-11-2-partc\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your model]\n\nPick a Florida organism, not the spoonbill. Name one part, tell what it does, and finish with a caption about a survival need. Want to draw it too? The sketch pad is a bonus.\n\n## Use your evidence · CER · g4sci-11-2-evidence\n\n[ON SCREEN: The quote]\n\nA classmate says drawings are just art, not science. Hmm!\n\n(Hold up a notebook page with a sketch and arrows.) Is this art, or is it explaining something?\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nPick your side in the planner, tap two pieces of evidence, then write your reasoning in your own words.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes.\n\n## Case closed · g4sci-11-2-closing\n\nCase closed for good! Draft C goes on the sign, because it explains, not just names.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your specimen]\n\nFinish your checklist and an annotated spoonbill sketch goes into your Field Notebook.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Confetti]\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next week]\n\nNext week: four trail cameras catch four Florida animals reacting at once. Why? Bring your detective eyes!\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s last word.\n","12-01":"# Lesson 12.01 teacher videos\n\n## Welcome · open the case · g4sci-12-1-welcome\n\nHi, scientists! Welcome to the Field Station. Today you're solving a real mystery.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CAM 1 beach · CAM 2 woods · CAM 3 river · CAM 4 marsh]\n\nThese pictures came from four trail cameras in Florida: a baby sea turtle, a panther, a manatee and a cottonmouth snake.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The times]\n\nLook at the clocks. All four animals moved within about two minutes, and the cameras were far apart. Hmm. I wonder why.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nThat's our case: what made them all react, and how did each one know?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your clue bar]\n\nSee \"My clues\" under the tabs? Every game you finish turns a question mark into a clue. Four clues cracks the case.\n\nType your first name on the ID card. Then press Listen on Dr. Reyes's walkie-talkie, right below me.\n\n## Warm-Up · the snap test · g4sci-12-1-warmup\n\nLet's start with you, because your body does this too.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The snap test]\n\nUnder the walkie-talkie is a game called \"Hear the snap.\" Tap the picture, then wait. Don't guess! When you hear the snap, tap fast. Try it three times. Watch me.\n\n(Do the test once on camera. Read your time out loud.)\n\nI didn't stop and think. My body just did it!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your steps]\n\nThen write what happened in your body, in order, from the sound to your tap. There are no wrong answers yet.\n\nHow did your body decide that fast? Press Listen and hear what Dr. Reyes thinks.\n\n## Word Wall · act it out · g4sci-12-1-vocab\n\nFour of today's words are steps, so let's act them out. Stand up if you can!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Stimulus]\n\nStimulus: something happens. (Snap your fingers.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Sense organ]\n\nSense organ: a body part notices it. (Point to your ear.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Brain]\n\nBrain: it decides. (Tap your head.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Response]\n\nResponse: you do something. (Turn around.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Act it out]\n\nFaster: snap, ear, head, turn! That's your snap test.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Word Lens]\n\nNow tap each word sticker below me. Dr. Reyes reads the word and what it means. Get a green check on all six.\n\n## The Lesson · what’s on this tab · g4sci-12-1-lesson\n\n[ON SCREEN: This tab]\n\nThis tab has four parts and three clues. Part 1 teaches the four steps, then you play Send the Signal for clue two. Part 2 is about different senses, with an alligator picture. In Part 3 you open Dr. Reyes's four cameras for clue three. And in Part 4, one more camera shows a heron. Sort its steps for clue four. Go in order, and watch your clue bar.\n\n## Part 1 · the four steps + Send the Signal · g4sci-12-1-part1\n\nLet's name what happened in your snap test.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The four steps]\n\nEvery reaction has four steps, in order. A stimulus: the snap. A sense organ notices it: your ears. Your brain decides: \"Something's behind me!\" And the response: you tapped. All four can happen in less than one second!\n\nThink about your warm-up list. Did you write a step for your brain? Lots of people skip it, because it's so fast.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Send the signal]\n\nBelow the reading is Send the Signal. Drag each step onto a station, starting out in the world. Get all four right and watch the signal run. That's clue two!\n\nFirst, press Listen. Dr. Reyes has something to say about the middle steps.\n\n## Part 2 · different senses · g4sci-12-1-part2\n\nAn eagle spots a fish with its eyes. A panther hears a rustle with its ears.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Five senses]\n\nEach sense organ picks up something different. Eyes pick up light, ears pick up sound, your nose picks up smell, your tongue picks up taste, and your skin picks up touch, heat and cold.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Watch out]\n\nWatch out for one mistake: the brain can't notice anything by itself. No sense organ, no signal. No signal, no response. Remember that for the end of the assignment.\n\n## The alligator picture · g4sci-12-1-figure\n\n[ON SCREEN: Alligator pathway]\n\nRead this picture left to right, like a sentence.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Read it left to right]\n\nA splash: that's the stimulus. The alligator's ear: the sense organ. Its brain decides, \"Food!\" Then it turns toward the splash: the response. The yellow lines show the message traveling. Can you say all four steps without looking?\n\n## Part 3 · open the four cameras (Clue 3) · g4sci-12-1-part3\n\nTime to look at the evidence, like a real field scientist.\n\n[ON SCREEN: How to open a camera]\n\nBelow the walkie-talkie are four trail cameras. Tap one, then press Next step to follow the message through that animal, one step at a time.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CAM 1 beach]\n\nFor each one, ask: what started this reaction? Was it the same thing for all four? That's clue three.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The manatee photo]\n\nThen tap the numbers on the manatee photo to see how it feels the water.\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes will tell you what to watch for.\n\n## Part 4 · the heron on CAM 5 (Clue 4) · g4sci-12-1-explore\n\n[ON SCREEN: The heron]\n\nCAM 5 caught a great blue heron hunting, one minute after the other cameras.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The heron story]\n\nRead the story slowly. The heron stands still. A fish swims by. The heron freezes, then strikes. I notice it froze first. I wonder why?\n\n[ON SCREEN: One card, sorted]\n\nDrag each card into its step. Let's do one together: \"A small fish swims into view.\" That happens out in the water, before the heron does anything. So it's the stimulus! You sort the rest.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The hidden step]\n\nThe pink box is the brain. No card goes there, because no camera can see inside the heron's head. Remember that for your CER.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes.\n\n## Self-Check · how it works · g4sci-12-1-quiz\n\n[ON SCREEN: Self-Check]\n\nThis Self-Check isn't graded. It's just for you. Answer one question, then press Next question. Picked a wrong answer? Read the hint. It tells you what to think about. Think about the four steps, and try again.\n\n## Assignment · crack the case + roadmap · g4sci-12-1-assignment\n\nYou made it to the assignment!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nFirst, crack the case. Still locked? Tap \"Show me where the clues are,\" then \"Go there.\" With all four clues, pick the answer that fits every clue. Did all four animals react to the same thing?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Today’s parts]\n\nThen there are three parts. Part B: two answers in complete sentences. Part C: make up your own Florida animal moment. A sketch is a bonus. And last, a CER.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Saved for you]\n\nEverything saves itself and shows up in Gathered Answers at the bottom. That's what you turn in.\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes wants a real field report.\n\n## Part B · short answers · g4sci-12-1-partb\n\n[ON SCREEN: Part B tools]\n\nAnswer three questions in complete sentences, using our step words. Under each box, the \"Your answer needs\" chips turn green as you include each part. Stuck? Press \"Need a hint?\" for one hint at a time. And \"My evidence\" adds anything you highlighted.\n\n[ON SCREEN: CAM 1 beach · CAM 2 woods]\n\nFor question five, pick an animal from last night's cameras. They're right above the question.\n\n## Part C · your own animal + sketch · g4sci-12-1-partc\n\nNow you're the scientist.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your scenario]\n\nMake up a short moment, like the heron story, about a Florida animal that wasn't in today's lesson. A pelican? A bobcat? A dolphin? Fill in the four boxes: what happened, what noticed it, what the brain decided, and what the animal did.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The sketch pad]\n\nIf you have time, sketch it too. It's a bonus. Draw with the pen. To add a label, pick Label, drag from a part of your drawing to an empty spot, and type. Add arrows to show the order.\n\n## Use your evidence · CER · g4sci-12-1-evidence\n\n[ON SCREEN: The classmate says]\n\nA classmate says, \"Animals just react. They don't really need a brain. Their senses do all the work.\" Hmm. Think about the hidden pink box from the heron. Do you agree?\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nRemember CER: your claim, your evidence from today's lesson, and your reasoning.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The planner]\n\nTap \"I agree\" or \"I disagree,\" then tap two pieces of evidence. They drop right into your boxes! The reasoning is all you. Try starting with \"This shows that…\"\n\n## Case closed · finish strong · g4sci-12-1-closing\n\n[ON SCREEN: Case closed · Confetti]\n\nCase closed! You did it, scientists.\n\nFour animals noticed four different things with four different senses, and each one's brain decided what to do. The four steps solved it.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Before you go]\n\nTwo last things. Check every box in What to Turn In to earn a heron feather. And download your field journal.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next time]\n\nPress Listen. Dr. Reyes has a sneak peek at next time.\n","12-02":"# Lesson 12.02 teacher videos\n\n## Welcome · the silent camera · g4sci-12-2-welcome\n\nWelcome back, scientists! The case isn’t over. Dr. Reyes put one more camera by the pond.\n\n[ON SCREEN: 9:41 PM · 9:52 PM]\n\nAt 9:41, moths are flying around the porch light. Eleven minutes later, they’re gone, and the camera saw nothing move. Hmm.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The printout]\n\nBut her bat detector heard something at that exact minute. Look: slow clicks, faster clicks, then a buzz. I wonder what that means.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nThat’s our case. Find four clues today. Press Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie, right below me.\n\n## Warm-Up · echo hunt (Clue 1) · g4sci-12-2-warmup\n\n(Clap once, then pretend to listen.) If I clap near a wall, the sound bounces back. That’s an echo.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Echo]\n\nBats use echoes to hunt. A bat clicks, and the echo off a moth comes back to its ears.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Test click]\n\nIn the echo hunt, press Click and read how long the echo took. A quicker echo means a closer moth. Use the test click to help you, then drag the pin to the moth. Find it three times.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes first.\n\n## Word Wall · g4sci-12-2-vocab\n\n[ON SCREEN: Pathway]\n\nA pathway is the path a message follows: stimulus, sense organ, brain, response. (Trace four steps in the air.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Model]\n\nA model shows how something works. Today you build pathway models.\n\nTap each word below me to hear it.\n\n## Part 1 · build a strong model (Clue 2) · g4sci-12-2-part1\n\n[ON SCREEN: The bonneted bat]\n\nMeet our suspect: the Florida bonneted bat, Florida’s biggest bat. Look at those ears!\n\n[ON SCREEN: A strong model]\n\nA strong model has four things: the four steps in order, an arrow between each step, the word signal on the arrow to the brain, and a caption about a survival need.\n\nBuild one for the bat in the game below. Watch the meter fill up as your model gets stronger.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## The eagle and bat picture · g4sci-12-2-figure\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two pathways]\n\nRead this picture like two sentences. Top row, the eagle: a fish flashes, the eye sees it, a signal goes to the brain, the eagle dives. Bottom row, the bat. Point to each step. Which step looks the most different?\n\n## Part 3 · match the sense (Clue 3) · g4sci-12-2-part3\n\n[ON SCREEN: Match]\n\nFour Florida animals, four different senses. Read what each one notices, then drag in the sense organ that can pick it up. When all four are done, look across. What stayed the same? Press Listen first.\n\n## Part 4 · fix the broken models (Clue 4) · g4sci-12-2-explore\n\n[ON SCREEN: Broken model]\n\nOne of the volunteers drew this. An echo bounces off a moth, and the bat grabs it. (Count on your fingers: one, two.) Hmm, only two steps. I notice something’s missing.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Run it]\n\nIn Part 4, pick what’s wrong, drag in the missing step, then press Run the model and watch the signal go all the way through. There are three broken models: the bat, the eagle and the bear.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Self-Check · g4sci-12-2-quiz\n\nQuick check: three questions, one at a time. It’s not graded. If you miss one, read the hint and try again.\n\n## Assignment · crack the case · g4sci-12-2-assignment\n\n[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nYou made it to the assignment! First, crack the case at the top. Still locked? Tap “Show me where the clues are.” Pick the answer that fits every clue.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your case report]\n\nThen three parts. Part B: two short answers. Part C: build two pathway models for two Florida animals that use different senses, then compare them. A sketch is a bonus. And last, your CER.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Part C · two pathway models · g4sci-12-2-partc\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two models]\n\nPick two Florida animals that use different senses, not the bat or the eagle. Maybe an owl that hears and a snake that smells? Write the four steps for each, then tell how they’re alike and different.\n\n## Use your evidence · CER · g4sci-12-2-evidence\n\n[ON SCREEN: The quote]\n\nA classmate says one model is enough to understand every animal. Hmm.\n\nThink back. Would a bat model tell you how a bear finds berries? Or how a crab feels the water?\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nPick your side in the planner, tap two pieces of evidence, then write your reasoning in your own words. Try starting with “This shows that…”\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes.\n\n## Case closed · g4sci-12-2-closing\n\n[ON SCREEN: Confetti]\n\nCase closed! A bat clicked, its ears caught the echoes, and its brain worked out where the moths were. Same four steps as the eagle, just a different sense.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your specimen]\n\nCheck every box in What to Turn In, and the bat detector printout goes into your Field Notebook.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next week]\n\nNext week: families! Why do baby animals look like their parents?\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s last word.\n","13-01":"# Lesson 13.01 teacher videos\n\n## Welcome · one parent, two gardens · g4sci-13-1-welcome\n\nWelcome, scientists! New week, new case.\n\n[ON SCREEN: The parent]\n\nThis is a wild beach sunflower on a Florida dune. Dr. Reyes collected its seeds.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Pot A · Pot B]\n\nShe planted some in the sun and some in the shade. Look how different they are! One is bushy with tons of flowers. One is tall and skinny. Hmm. I wonder… did the seeds get mixed up?\n\n[ON SCREEN: The case question]\n\nThat’s our case. Find four clues today. Press Listen on Dr. Reyes’s walkie-talkie, right below me.\n\n## Warm-Up · find the family (Clue 1) · g4sci-13-1-warmup\n\n[ON SCREEN: Baby]\n\nLook at this baby. Spots! Blue eyes!\n\n[ON SCREEN: Parent]\n\nNow look at its mom: plain tan, no spots. Same face, same round ears, though. This kitten is a Florida panther, and its spots fade as it grows.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Look for]\n\nIn the warm-up game, match four Florida babies to their parents. Look at body shape first, because colors can fool you. Then list the traits the baby inherited.\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes first.\n\n## Word Wall · g4sci-13-1-vocab\n\n[ON SCREEN: Inherited]\n\nInherited means passed down from your parents. (Point from yourself back over your shoulder.)\n\n[ON SCREEN: Environment]\n\nEnvironment is where you live and grow. (Spread your arms wide.)\n\nTap each word below me to hear it.\n\n## Part 2 · grow the twins (Clue 2) · g4sci-13-1-part2\n\n(Hold up two fingers.) Two seeds. Same parent.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Two pots]\n\nIn Grow the twins, you pick the garden for each pot: sun or shade, lots of water or a little. Then press Grow.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Watch for]\n\nYour job is to notice. Which traits change when the garden changes? And which ones never change, no matter what? Make sure one pot grows in the shade.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Part 3 · stamp the case (Clue 3) · g4sci-13-1-part3\n\n[ON SCREEN: Three stamps]\n\nFour Florida cases, three stamps. Read the facts, stamp each one, then pick the fact that proves it. Watch out: two of them need the BOTH stamp.\n\n## Part 4 · pass it on? (Clue 4) · g4sci-13-1-explore\n\n[ON SCREEN: The mother]\n\nThis panther mom was caught on a trail camera, carrying her kitten. She just had a litter.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Guess]\n\nShe has a kinked tail. A scar on her nose. Round ears. And she’s thin after a hard year. (Tap your chin.) Which of those will her kittens get?\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next generation]\n\nGuess for each trait, then press Next generation. A wrong guess is fine. That’s how scientists learn.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Self-Check · g4sci-13-1-quiz\n\nQuick check: three questions, one at a time. It’s not graded. If you miss one, read the hint and try again.\n\n## Assignment · crack the case · g4sci-13-1-assignment\n\n[ON SCREEN: Crack the case]\n\nYou made it! First, crack the case at the top. Still locked? Tap “Show me where the clues are.” Pick the answer that fits every clue.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your case report]\n\nThen three parts. Part B: two short answers. Part C: pick a real parent and its offspring, maybe your pet or a backyard plant. Name three inherited traits and two from the environment. A sketch is a bonus. Last, your CER.\n\nPress Listen on the walkie-talkie.\n\n## Part C · parent and offspring · g4sci-13-1-partc\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your pair]\n\nPick a real parent and offspring. A dog and her puppy? A mango tree and its seedling? Name three inherited traits and two from the environment, then tell how you can tell the difference.\n\n## Use your evidence · CER · g4sci-13-1-evidence\n\n[ON SCREEN: The quote]\n\nA classmate says babies are clones of their parents. A clone is an exact copy. Hmm.\n\nThink back. Were the two sunflower pots exact copies? Did the kittens look exactly like their mom?\n\n[ON SCREEN: CER]\n\nPick your side in the planner, tap two pieces of evidence, then write your reasoning in your own words. Try starting with “This shows that…”\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes.\n\n## Case closed · g4sci-13-1-closing\n\n[ON SCREEN: Confetti]\n\nCase closed! Same parent, different gardens. The flower color and the leaves came from the parent. The sun did the rest.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Your specimen]\n\nCheck every box in What to Turn In, and a seed packet goes into your Field Notebook.\n\n[ON SCREEN: Next lesson]\n\nNext time: how did one flower make all those seeds?\n\nPress Listen for Dr. Reyes’s last word.\n"},"pics":{"oak":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","owl":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","fallen":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","fungus":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","liveoak":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aerial":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","springs":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","manatee":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","python":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","beak":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","sweep":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","legs":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","lagoon":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","gator":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","draftA":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","draftB":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","draftC":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","anhinga":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","cam1":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","cam2":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","cam3":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","cam4":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","heron":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","pathway":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","moth":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","empty":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","detector":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","bat":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","pathways":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","parent":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","sun":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","shade":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","kitten":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","panther":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","mom":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","kits":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"},"picCaps":{"oak":"BEFORE · the big live oak · June","owl":"BEFORE · barred owl up in the limbs · June","fallen":"AFTER · the oak knocked over · August","fungus":"AFTER · mushrooms on the trunk · October","liveoak":"Southern live oak with resurrection ferns (CC0)","aerial":"Manatees from the air · Crystal River (USFWS)","springs":"Manatees crowd into a warm spring in winter (USFWS)","manatee":"A Florida manatee (USFWS)","python":"A Burmese python in the Everglades (USGS)","beak":"pg. 3 · the beak · flat and wide at the tip","sweep":"pg. 4 · beak in the water","legs":"pg. 5 · the legs","lagoon":"pg. 6 · where I found it","gator":"An American alligator hiding (CC BY-SA 3.0)","draftA":"Draft A · a photo with labels","draftB":"Draft B · a painting from 1836 (Audubon)","draftC":"Draft C · a photo with arrows and a caption","anhinga":"The anhinga model","cam1":"CAM 1 · beach · 9:14:08 PM · sea turtle hatchling","cam2":"CAM 2 · woods · 9:14:31 PM · Florida panther","cam3":"CAM 3 · river · 9:15:02 PM · Florida manatee","cam4":"CAM 4 · marsh · 9:15:47 PM · cottonmouth","heron":"Great blue heron, Everglades National Park (NPS)","pathway":"An alligator hears a splash: the four-step pathway","moth":"CAM 6 · 9:41 PM · moths at the porch light","empty":"CAM 6 · 9:52 PM · no moths","detector":"Bat detector printout · 9:52 PM","bat":"Florida bonneted bat (Shalana Gray, CC BY-SA 4.0)","pathways":"Eagle and bat pathways","parent":"Tag P · the wild parent plant on the dunes","sun":"Pot A · sunny dune garden","shade":"Pot B · shady courtyard","kitten":"Florida panther kitten (USACE, public domain)","panther":"Adult Florida panther (USFWS, public domain)","mom":"Panther mother on a trail camera (USFWS, public domain)","kits":"Panther kittens in their den (USFWS, public domain)"}};
